### Key design decisions

1. **Branded IDs**: `PostId`, `AuthorId`, and `TagId` are all strings at runtime, but the compiler won't let you pass an `AuthorId` where a `PostId` is expected.

2. **Tags as objects, not strings**: Separating `name` (display) from `slug` (URL) avoids problems with casing and spaces. If you want something simpler, `tags: string[]` works fine for small projects.

3. **Body as a discriminated union**: Making the format explicit (`markdown`, `html`, `blocks`) means renderers can `switch` on `format` exhaustively. You can add new formats later without breaking existing code.

4. **Status as a discriminated union**: This makes invalid states unrepresentable. A published post must have `publishedAt`, and a draft can't have one. Compare that with `status: string; publishedAt?: string`, which allows contradictions.

5. **`AuthorSummary` embedded in the post**: The post carries only what's needed to render a byline. The full `Author` lives separately, which keeps payloads small.

6. **Separate input types**: `CreateBlogPostInput` omits server-managed fields (`id`, timestamps, status) and takes IDs instead of full objects, which matches how APIs typically receive data.

7. **Dates as ISO strings**: These serialize cleanly over JSON. Convert them to `Date` at the edges if needed.

If you're validating at runtime (e.g., API input), consider defining these with **Zod** and deriving the types via `z.infer<typeof schema>` so validation and types stay in sync.

Here's a data model that stays simple at its core and leaves room to extend:

```typescript
// ---------- Branded ID types ----------
// Prevents accidentally passing an AuthorId where a PostId is expected.
type Brand<T, B extends string> = T & { readonly __brand: B };

export type PostId = Brand<string, "PostId">;
export type AuthorId = Brand<string, "AuthorId">;
export type TagId = Brand<string, "TagId">;

/** ISO 8601 timestamp, e.g. "2024-05-01T12:00:00Z" */
export type ISODateString = Brand<string, "ISODateString">;

/** URL-safe identifier, e.g. "my-first-post" */
export type Slug = Brand<string, "Slug">;

// ---------- Author ----------
export interface Author {
  id: AuthorId;
  name: string;
  slug: Slug;
  email?: string;
  avatarUrl?: string;
  bio?: string;
}

/** Lightweight author reference embedded in posts (avoids over-fetching). */
export type AuthorSummary = Pick<Author, "id" | "name" | "slug" | "avatarUrl">;

// ---------- Tag ----------
export interface Tag {
  id: TagId;
  name: string; // Display name: "TypeScript"
  slug: Slug;   // URL form: "typescript"
}

// ---------- Body ----------
// A discriminated union so the renderer knows how to interpret content.
export type PostBody =
  | { format: "markdown"; content: string }
  | { format: "html"; content: string }
  | { format: "blocks"; blocks: ContentBlock[] };

export type ContentBlock =
  | { type: "paragraph"; text: string }
  | { type: "heading"; level: 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6; text: string }
  | { type: "image"; src: string; alt: string; caption?: string }
  | { type: "code"; language?: string; code: string }
  | { type: "quote"; text: string; attribution?: string }
  | { type: "list"; ordered: boolean; items: string[] };

// ---------- Post status ----------
// Modeled as a union so `publishedAt` exists only when it makes sense.
export type PostStatus =
  | { state: "draft" }
  | { state: "scheduled"; publishAt: ISODateString }
  | { state: "published"; publishedAt: ISODateString }
  | { state: "archived"; publishedAt: ISODateString; archivedAt: ISODateString };

// ---------- Blog Post ----------
export interface BlogPost {
  id: PostId;
  title: string;
  slug: Slug;
  excerpt?: string;
  author: AuthorSummary;
  tags: Tag[];
  body: PostBody;
  status: PostStatus;
  createdAt: ISODateString;
  updatedAt: ISODateString;
}

// ---------- Input types (for create/update operations) ----------
export interface CreateBlogPostInput {
  title: string;
  slug?: string;          // Generated from title if omitted
  excerpt?: string;
  authorId: AuthorId;     // Reference by ID, not embedded object
  tagIds: TagId[];
  body: PostBody;
}

export type UpdateBlogPostInput = Partial<Omit<CreateBlogPostInput, "authorId">>;
```

### Example usage

```typescript
const post: BlogPost = {
  id: "post_123" as PostId,
  title: "Designing Type-Safe Data Models",
  slug: "designing-type-safe-data-models" as Slug,
  author: {
    id: "auth_1" as AuthorId,
    name: "Ada Lovelace",
    slug: "ada-lovelace" as Slug,
  },
  tags: [{ id: "tag_ts" as TagId, name: "TypeScript", slug: "typescript" as Slug }],
  body: { format: "markdown", content: "# Hello\n\nTypes are great." },
  status: { state: "published", publishedAt: "2024-05-01T12:00:00Z" as ISODateString },
  createdAt: "2024-04-28T09:00:00Z" as ISODateString,
  updatedAt: "2024-05-01T12:00:00Z" as ISODateString,
};

// Discriminated unions give safe narrowing:
function publishDate(p: BlogPost): string | null {
  switch (p.status.state) {
    case "published":
    case "archived":
      return p.status.publishedAt; // TypeScript knows this exists
    default:
      return null;
  }
}
```

### Key design decisions

| Decision | Rationale |
|---|---|
| **Branded IDs** | Catch mix-ups like `getPost(authorId)` at compile time. |
| **Tags as objects, not `string[]`** | Allows renaming a tag without breaking URLs, and supports a separate display name and slug. Use `string[]` if you want maximum simplicity. |
| **`AuthorSummary` embedded** | Posts usually need only the name and avatar, not the full profile. |
| **Body as a discriminated union** | Supports Markdown today and lets you migrate to structured blocks later without a breaking change. |
| **Status as a union** | Makes illegal states unrepresentable. A draft can't have a `publishedAt`. |
| **Separate input types** | Clients send IDs; the server returns hydrated objects. Server-managed fields (`id`, timestamps) aren't client-settable. |

