## Design Decisions

| Decision | Rationale |
|---|---|
| **Branded IDs** | Stops you from passing an `AuthorId` where a `PostId` is expected. There is no runtime cost. |
| **`AuthorSummary` embedded in post** | Posts usually render the author's name and avatar, not their email or full bio. This keeps payloads small and avoids leaking private fields. |
| **`Tag` as an object, not `string[]`** | Separating `name` from `slug` lets you rename a tag's display text without breaking URLs. IDs allow normalization in the database. |
| **`PostBody` with a `format` discriminator** | Renderers can switch on format safely. You can migrate formats, such as moving from markdown to blocks, without ambiguity. |
| **`PostStatus` as a discriminated union** | Invalid states can't be represented. A `draft` has no `publishedAt`, and a `scheduled` post must have a `publishAt`. |
| **Separate `Create`/`Update` inputs** | Clients shouldn't send `id`, `createdAt`, or a fully resolved author object. The input types reflect what the API accepts. |
| **`readonly` on immutable fields** | `id` and `createdAt` never change after creation. |

