| **`AuthorSummary` embedded in posts** | Avoids carrying sensitive or heavy author data (email, bio) everywhere. |
| **Body as a discriminated union** | Supports Markdown, HTML, or structured blocks without breaking changes later. |
| **Status as a discriminated union** | Makes illegal states unrepresentable, e.g. a draft can't have a `publishedAt`. |
| **Separate input types** | The client shouldn't supply `id`, `createdAt`, or full author objects. |
| **ISO strings for dates** | They serialize cleanly to and from JSON. Convert to `Date` at the edges if needed. |

**Simpler alternative:** If this is for a small project, you can drop the branded types and use `tags: string[]` and `body: string`. The structure above is meant to scale as requirements grow.

Here's a layered data model that separates the core entities, supports drafts and publishing, and keeps the types safe to use across your app.

## Core Types

```typescript
// ---------- Branded IDs (prevent mixing up string IDs) ----------
type Brand<T, B extends string> = T & { readonly __brand: B };

export type PostId = Brand<string, "PostId">;
export type AuthorId = Brand<string, "AuthorId">;
export type TagId = Brand<string, "TagId">;

/** ISO 8601 timestamp, e.g. "2024-05-01T12:00:00Z" */
export type ISODateString = Brand<string, "ISODateString">;

/** URL-safe identifier, e.g. "my-first-post" */
export type Slug = Brand<string, "Slug">;

// ---------- Author ----------
export interface Author {
  id: AuthorId;
  name: string;
  slug: Slug;
  email?: string;
  bio?: string;
  avatarUrl?: string;
  links?: {
    website?: string;
    twitter?: string;
    github?: string;
  };
}

/** Lightweight author reference embedded in post listings */
export type AuthorSummary = Pick<Author, "id" | "name" | "slug" | "avatarUrl">;

// ---------- Tag ----------
export interface Tag {
  id: TagId;
  name: string;        // Display name: "TypeScript"
  slug: Slug;          // URL form: "typescript"
  description?: string;
}

// ---------- Body ----------
/**
 * Body stored with its format so renderers know how to handle it.
 * A discriminated union makes adding formats (e.g. rich-text JSON) safe.
 */
export type PostBody =
  | { format: "markdown"; content: string }
  | { format: "html"; content: string }
  | { format: "richtext"; content: RichTextNode[] };

export interface RichTextNode {
  type: string;                    // "paragraph", "heading", "image", ...
  attrs?: Record<string, unknown>;
  text?: string;
  children?: RichTextNode[];
}

// ---------- Post ----------
interface PostBase {
  id: PostId;
  title: string;
  slug: Slug;
  excerpt?: string;
  body: PostBody;
  authorId: AuthorId;
  coAuthorIds?: AuthorId[];
  tagIds: TagId[];
  coverImage?: {
    url: string;
    alt: string;
  };
  createdAt: ISODateString;
  updatedAt: ISODateString;
}

/** Status-dependent fields enforced via discriminated union */
export type Post =
  | (PostBase & { status: "draft" })
  | (PostBase & { status: "scheduled"; scheduledFor: ISODateString })
  | (PostBase & { status: "published"; publishedAt: ISODateString })
  | (PostBase & { status: "archived"; publishedAt: ISODateString; archivedAt: ISODateString });

export type PostStatus = Post["status"];
```

## View / API Shapes

The stored model uses IDs; views usually need resolved relations.

```typescript
/** Fully resolved post for rendering a single post page */
export type PostWithRelations = Omit<Post, "authorId" | "coAuthorIds" | "tagIds"> & {
  author: AuthorSummary;
  coAuthors: AuthorSummary[];
  tags: Tag[];
  readingTimeMinutes: number; // derived
};

/** Compact shape for list/index pages (no body) */
export interface PostSummary {
  id: PostId;
  title: string;
  slug: Slug;
  excerpt?: string;
  author: AuthorSummary;
  tags: Pick<Tag, "name" | "slug">[];
  publishedAt: ISODateString;
  coverImage?: Post["coverImage"];
}
```

## Input Types (Create / Update)

```typescript
/** Server assigns id, timestamps, and slug (if omitted) */
export interface CreatePostInput {
  title: string;
  slug?: string;
  excerpt?: string;
  body: PostBody;
  authorId: AuthorId;
  coAuthorIds?: AuthorId[];
  tagIds?: TagId[];
  coverImage?: { url: string; alt: string };
}

/** All fields optional; status changes go through dedicated actions */
export type UpdatePostInput = Partial<CreatePostInput>;

export type PostStatusTransition =
  | { action: "publish" }
  | { action: "schedule"; scheduledFor: ISODateString }
  | { action: "unpublish" }   // -> draft
  | { action: "archive" };
```

## Example Usage

```typescript
function getPublishDate(post: Post): string | null {
  switch (post.status) {
    case "published":
    case "archived":
      return post.publishedAt;      // ✅ type-safe access
    case "scheduled":
      return post.scheduledFor;
    case "draft":
      return null;                  // ✅ publishedAt doesn't exist here
  }
}

function isMarkdown(body: PostBody): body is Extract<PostBody, { format: "markdown" }> {
  return body.format === "markdown";
}
```

## Design Decisions

| Decision | Rationale |
|---|---|
| **Branded IDs** | Prevents passing an `AuthorId` where a `PostId` is expected. This is a compile-time check with zero runtime cost. |
| **Tags as entities, not `string[]`** | Allows renaming, descriptions, and stable slugs. Use `string[]` instead if you want simplicity. |
| **Discriminated union on `status`** | Makes invalid states unrepresentable, e.g. a draft with a `publishedAt`. |
| **Body includes `format`** | Lets you switch or support multiple editors without ambiguity. |
| **IDs in storage, objects in views** | Normalized storage avoids duplicated author/tag data. `PostWithRelations` serves the UI. |
| **Separate input types** | Clients can't set `id`, `createdAt`, etc. Status changes are explicit actions. |

