Here are the TypeScript types for a product page, designed with common e-commerce best practices in mind (such as handling currencies safely and supporting different rich-text formats):

```ts
export interface ProductImage {
  id: string;
  url: string;
  alt: string;
  width?: number;
  height?: number;
  isPrimary?: boolean;
}

export interface ProductPrice {
  /** Price in minor units (e.g., cents) to avoid floating-point rounding issues */
  amount: number;
  /** ISO 4217 currency code (e.g., 'USD', 'EUR', 'GBP') */
  currency: string;
  /** Optional pre-formatted string for display (e.g., '$29.99') */
  formatted?: string;
  /** Optional original price if the product is on sale */
  compareAtAmount?: number;
}

/**
 * Supports common ways CMSs and APIs return formatted text.
 */
export type FormattedDescription =
  | { format: 'html'; content: string }
  | { format: 'markdown'; content: string }
  | { format: 'blocks'; content: RichTextBlock[] };

export type RichTextBlock =
  | { type: 'paragraph'; text: string }
  | { type: 'heading'; level: 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6; text: string }
  | { type: 'list'; style: 'ordered' | 'unordered'; items: string[] }
  | { type: 'quote'; text: string; attribution?: string };

export interface ProductPageData {
  id: string;
  slug: string;
  name: string;
  price: ProductPrice;
  images: ProductImage[];
  longDescription: FormattedDescription;
}
```

### Key Design Choices
* **`ProductPrice`**: Uses an object instead of a plain `number` so you can track currency and avoid floating-point math issues by storing `amount` in cents.
* **`FormattedDescription`**: Uses a discriminated union so you can safely narrow the type depending on whether your backend/CMS returns raw HTML, Markdown, or structured rich-text blocks.
* **`ProductImage`**: Includes `alt` as a required field to enforce accessibility.

Here is a clean, production-ready set of TypeScript types for a product page.

