# Product Page TypeScript Types

```typescript
// Rich text formatting for the long description
type RichTextNode = {
  type: 'paragraph' | 'heading' | 'list' | 'quote' | 'code';
  content: string;
  children?: RichTextNode[];
};

type DescriptionBlock =
  | { kind: 'text'; value: string }
  | { kind: 'heading'; level: 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6; value: string }
  | { kind: 'list'; ordered: boolean; items: string[] }
  | { kind: 'image'; src: string; alt?: string }
  | { kind: 'html'; value: string }; // sanitized HTML

// Image with responsive variants
type ProductImage = {
  id: string;
  url: string;
  altText: string;
  width: number;
  height: number;
  isPrimary?: boolean;
};

type ProductPage = {
  id: string;
  slug: string;
  name: string;
  price: {
    amount: number;        // e.g. 19.99
    currency: string;      // e.g. "USD"
    compareAtPrice?: number; // original price if discounted
  };
  images: ProductImage[];
  description: DescriptionBlock[]; // or RichTextNode[]
  descriptionHtml?: string; // alternative: pre-rendered HTML
  createdAt: Date;
  updatedAt: Date;
};
```

## Notes on the two description approaches

1. **Structured blocks** (`DescriptionBlock[]`) — safest, portable, easy to render with React components, avoids XSS from raw HTML.

2. **Pre-rendered HTML string** (`descriptionHtml`) — common with headless CMSs (e.g. Shopify, Contentful), but you must sanitize it (e.g. with DOMPurify) before injecting.

Pick based on where the content comes from — CMS integrations usually push you toward `descriptionHtml`, while custom apps benefit from structured blocks.

