export type PostStatus = "draft" | "published" | "archived";

export interface BlogPost {
  id: PostId;
  title: string;
  /** URL-friendly identifier, unique per post */
  slug: string;
  author: Author;
  tags: string[];
  /** Markdown source. Swap for a structured block type if you need rich embeds. */
  body: string;
  /** Short plain-text summary for listings and SEO. */
  excerpt?: string;
  coverImageUrl?: string;
  status: PostStatus;
  createdAt: ISODateString;
  updatedAt: ISODateString;
  publishedAt?: ISODateString;
  /** Estimated read time in minutes. */
  readingTimeMinutes?: number;
}
```

## Design notes

- **`Author` as an object, not a string.** Even if you only need a name today, posts almost always end up needing an avatar, link, or bio. Keeping it nested avoids a breaking change later. If you're storing posts in a relational DB, you'd typically store `authorId` and join — the type can still expose the full `Author` at the API boundary.
- **Branded IDs.** `PostId` and `UserId` are both strings at runtime, but the compiler stops you from mixing them up. Cheap to add, catches real bugs.
- **`slug` separate from `id`.** IDs are for the database; slugs are for URLs. They change independently.
- **`status` + `publishedAt`.** A draft has no `publishedAt`; a published post does. This lets you filter drafts out of public queries without deleting them.
- **`body` as a Markdown string.** Simplest thing that works. If you need embedded components (code sandboxes, tweets, images with captions), replace it with a discriminated union of blocks:

```ts
export type PostBlock =
  | { type: "paragraph"; text: string }
  | { type: "heading"; level: 1 | 2 | 3; text: string }
  | { type: "code"; language: string; code: string }
  | { type: "image"; url: string; alt: string; caption?: string };

// then: body: PostBlock[];
```

- **`tags: string[]`.** Fine for a small blog. If tags get their own pages, promote them to `Tag { id, name, slug }` and reference by ID.

