# Blog Post Data Model (TypeScript)

Here's a well-structured model with some thoughtful choices baked in:

```typescript
/** ISO 8601 datetime string, e.g. "2025-01-15T10:30:00.000Z" */
type ISODateString = string;

/** URL-safe identifier, e.g. "my-first-post" */
type Slug = string;

/** Lowercase tag identifier, e.g. "typescript" */
type Tag = string;

export interface Author {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  /** Optional URL to avatar image */
  avatarUrl?: string;
  /** Optional short bio shown at the end of the post */
  bio?: string;
  /** Optional handle for linking, e.g. "@jane" */
  handle?: string;
}

export type PostStatus = 'draft' | 'published' | 'archived';

export interface BlogPost {
  id: string;
  slug: Slug;
  title: string;
  /** Optional short description / excerpt for previews and SEO */
  summary?: string;
  author: Author;
  tags: Tag[];
  /** Markdown or plain text body */
  body: string;
  status: PostStatus;
  createdAt: ISODateString;
  updatedAt: ISODateString;
  /** Set when status transitions to "published" */
  publishedAt?: ISODateString;
  /** Estimated reading time in minutes (derived) */
  readingTimeMinutes?: number;
  /** Optional cover image for social/preview cards */
  coverImageUrl?: string;
}
```

## Design Notes

**Key decisions and why:**

- **`id` vs `slug`**: Both are useful — `id` is stable (never changes even if the title is edited), `slug` is human/SEO-friendly and may change. Keeping them separate avoids breaking URLs vs. breaking references.

- **`Author` as a nested object** rather than just a `string`: This scales when you need avatars, bios, or multiple authors. If you're storing in a relational DB, you might instead store `authorId: string` and resolve it separately — the nested form here is convenient for API responses.

- **`tags: Tag[]`** (string array) rather than objects: Tags are usually flat labels. If they need metadata (color, description), promote them to an object.

- **`status` discriminated union** instead of a boolean `published`: Booleans don't age well. `'draft' | 'published' | 'archived'` covers the common lifecycle and makes UI branching explicit.

- **Optional fields marked with `?`** rather than `| null`: Pick one convention and stick to it. `?` means "not present"; if your backend returns `null`, use `field: T | null` instead. Mixing both is a common source of bugs.

- **`ISODateString` as a string alias**: Blog posts almost always cross a JSON boundary, where dates become strings anyway. Aliasing keeps the intent clear without pretending it's a `Date`.

- **`readingTimeMinutes` is derived**, not user-authored — a comment notes this so nobody tries to set it manually.

## Optional: Runtime Validation

If you're parsing untrusted input, pair this with a schema validator (Zod, Valibot, etc.):

```typescript
import { z } from 'zod';

const BlogPostSchema = z.object({
  id: z.string(),
  slug: z.string().regex(/^[a-z0-9-]+$/),
  title: z.string().min(1),
  summary: z.string().optional(),
  author: z.object({
    id: z.string(),
    name: z.string(),
    avatarUrl: z.string().url().optional(),
    bio: z.string().optional(),
    handle: z.string().optional(),
  }),
  tags: z.array(z.string().min(1)),
  body: z.string(),
  status: z.enum(['draft', 'published', 'archived']),
  createdAt: z.string().datetime(),
  updatedAt: z.string().datetime(),
  publishedAt: z.string().datetime().optional(),
  readingTimeMinutes: z.number().int().positive().optional(),
  coverImageUrl: z.string().url().optional(),
});

export type BlogPost = z.infer<typeof BlogPostSchema>;
```

This way the TypeScript type and the runtime validator can never drift apart.

# Blog Post Data Model

Here's a TypeScript data model for a blog post. I've split it into a few composable types rather than one flat interface — this makes it easier to reuse pieces (e.g., `Author` on comments, `Tag` in filters) and to keep the "stored" shape separate from the "rendered" shape.

```ts
// ---------- Identifiers ----------
// Branded types prevent accidentally passing a UserId where a PostId is expected.
export type PostId = string & { readonly __brand: 'PostId' };
export type UserId = string & { readonly __brand: 'UserId' };

// ---------- Author ----------
export interface Author {
  id: UserId;
  name: string;
  /** Optional handle, e.g. "@ada" — no leading '@' stored. */
  handle?: string;
  avatarUrl?: string;
  bio?: string;
}

// ---------- Tags ----------
// A tag is just a slug + display label. Slugs are lowercase, kebab-case,
// and are what you'd use in URLs or query params.
export interface Tag {
  slug: string;      // "typescript"
  label: string;     // "TypeScript"
}

// ---------- Body ----------
// Store the body as structured content, not raw HTML. This keeps you safe
// from XSS and lets you render to multiple targets (web, RSS, email).
export type PostBody =
  | { format: 'markdown'; content: string }
  | { format: 'portable-text'; content: unknown[] } // e.g. from Sanity/Notion
  | { format: 'html'; content: string };            // only if you trust the source

// ---------- Post status ----------
export type PostStatus = 'draft' | 'published' | 'archived';

// ---------- The main model ----------
export interface BlogPost {
  id: PostId;
  slug: string;              // URL-safe identifier, e.g. "designing-a-data-model"
  title: string;
  /** Short summary used for previews, cards, and meta description. */
  excerpt?: string;
  author: Author;
  tags: Tag[];
  body: PostBody;

  status: PostStatus;
  /** ISO 8601 timestamps. */
  createdAt: string;
  updatedAt: string;
  /** Set when status transitions to 'published'. */
  publishedAt?: string;

  /** Optional cover image for cards / open graph. */
  coverImageUrl?: string;
  /** Estimated read time in minutes — usually derived, but cached here. */
  readingTimeMinutes?: number;
}
```

## Design notes

- **`body` is a discriminated union** rather than a plain `string`. You can render Markdown, Portable Text (Sanity/Notion), or pre-sanitized HTML without changing the surrounding type. The `format` field acts as the discriminant.
- **`Tag` is an object, not a `string[]`.** A slug/label pair lets you display "TypeScript" while linking to `/tags/typescript`. If you truly only need slugs, `tags: string[]` is fine.
- **`Author` is embedded, not an ID.** For a blog this is usually fine — the author data is small and rarely changes. If authors have their own lifecycle, swap it for `authorId: UserId` and resolve separately.
- **Timestamps are strings, not `Date`.** This matches what JSON APIs return and avoids timezone confusion at the boundary. Convert to `Date` in the UI layer.
- **`status` + `publishedAt`** are separate so you can schedule posts and know *when* a post went live even if it's later archived.
- **Branded IDs** (`PostId`, `UserId`) are a small trick that catches real bugs — passing a `UserId` where a `PostId` is expected becomes a type error.

