# Product Page TypeScript Types

Here's a set of types covering a product page with structured rich-text descriptions. The description uses a block-based approach (similar to Portable Text / Sanity / Notion) rather than raw HTML, which keeps the data safe to render and easy to transform.

```ts
// ---------- Shared primitives ----------

/** ISO 4217 currency code, e.g. "USD", "EUR". */
export type CurrencyCode = string;

/** Money is stored in minor units (cents) to avoid float rounding. */
export interface Money {
  amount: number;          // e.g. 1999 for $19.99
  currency: CurrencyCode;
}

export type ImageFormat = "jpg" | "png" | "webp" | "avif";

export interface ProductImage {
  id: string;
  url: string;
  alt: string;
  width: number;
  height: number;
  format?: ImageFormat;
  /** Optional responsive variants keyed by width. */
  srcSet?: Array<{ url: string; width: number }>;
}

// ---------- Rich text (long description) ----------

export type TextMark =
  | "strong"
  | "em"
  | "underline"
  | "code"
  | "strikethrough";

export interface LinkMark {
  type: "link";
  href: string;
  /** Open in new tab, rel="noopener noreferrer". */
  external?: boolean;
}

export type MarkDef = LinkMark;

/** Inline span of text with optional marks and mark definitions. */
export interface RichTextSpan {
  type: "span";
  text: string;
  /** Either a plain TextMark string or a key into `markDefs`. */
  marks?: Array<TextMark | string>;
}

export interface InlineImage {
  type: "image";
  image: ProductImage;
}

/** Anything that can appear inside a block's children. */
export type InlineNode = RichTextSpan | InlineImage;

export interface BlockBase {
  /** Stable key for React reconciliation. */
  _key: string;
  markDefs?: MarkDef[];
}

export interface ParagraphBlock extends BlockBase {
  type: "paragraph";
  children: InlineNode[];
}

export interface HeadingBlock extends BlockBase {
  type: "heading";
  level: 2 | 3 | 4;
  children: InlineNode[];
}

export interface ListBlock extends BlockBase {
  type: "list";
  style: "bullet" | "number";
  items: Array<{
    _key: string;
    children: InlineNode[];
  }>;
}

export interface QuoteBlock extends BlockBase {
  type: "quote";
  children: InlineNode[];
  attribution?: string;
}

export interface CodeBlock extends BlockBase {
  type: "code";
  language?: string;
  code: string;
}

export interface DividerBlock extends BlockBase {
  type: "divider";
}

export interface TableBlock extends BlockBase {
  type: "table";
  headers: string[];
  rows: string[][];
}

/** Discriminated union of every block allowed in a description. */
export type RichTextBlock =
  | ParagraphBlock
  | HeadingBlock
  | ListBlock
  | QuoteBlock
  | CodeBlock
  | DividerBlock
  | TableBlock;

export interface RichText {
  blocks: RichTextBlock[];
}

// ---------- Product ----------

export type ProductStatus = "draft" | "active" | "archived";

export interface ProductVariant {
  id: string;
  sku: string;
  name: string;                 // e.g. "Large / Blue"
  price: Money;
  compareAtPrice?: Money;       // for showing strikethrough
  inStock: boolean;
  imageId?: string;             // ref into Product.images
}

export interface ProductPage {
  id: string;
  slug: string;
  name: string;

  /** Short tagline shown under the name. */
  tagline?: string;

  price: Money;
  compareAtPrice?: Money;

  images: ProductImage[];

  /** Long-form, formatted description. */
  description: RichText;

  variants?: ProductVariant[];

  status: ProductStatus;
  createdAt: string;            // ISO 8601
  updatedAt: string;            // ISO 8601
}
```

## Why this shape?

- **`Money` with minor units** — storing `1999` instead of `19.99` avoids floating-point surprises when you compute totals or discounts.
- **`srcSet` on images** — lets you serve responsive images without inventing a URL scheme in the component.
- **Discriminated union for blocks** — the `type` field narrows cleanly in a `switch`, so a renderer can't accidentally treat a heading as a paragraph.
- **`markDefs` + marks** — matches the Portable Text convention: a span references `markDefs` by key when it needs metadata (e.g. a link's `href`), and uses bare strings for simple marks like `"strong"`.
- **`_key` on blocks** — stable IDs make React lists happy without falling back to array indices.
- **`imageId` on variants** — lets you reuse the main image array rather than duplicating URLs per variant.

## Example data

```ts
const example: ProductPage = {
  id: "prod_123",
  slug: "linen-shirt",
  name: "Linen Shirt",
  tagline: "Breathable, pre-washed, made in Portugal.",
  price: { amount: 8900, currency: "USD" },
  compareAtPrice: { amount: 12000, currency: "USD" },
  status: "active",
  createdAt: "2024-03-01T10:00:00Z",
  updatedAt: "2024-04-12T08:30:00Z",
  images: [
    { id: "img_1", url: "/linen-1.jpg", alt: "Front view", width: 1200, height: 1600 },
  ],
  description: {
    blocks: [
      {
        _key: "b1",
        type: "heading",
        level: 2,
        children: [{ type: "span", text: "Fabric & fit" }],
      },
      {
        _key: "b2",
        type: "paragraph",
        markDefs: [{ type: "link", href: "/sizing", external: false }],
        children: [
          { type: "span", text: "Cut for a " },
          { type: "span", text: "relaxed", marks: ["em"] },
          { type: "span", text: " fit. See our " },
          { type: "span", text: "sizing guide", marks: ["linkKey"] },
          { type: "span", text: "." },
        ],
      },
    ],
  },
};
```

If you're using a headless CMS (Sanity, Contentful, Payload), you can typically map its rich-text output straight onto `RichTextBlock` with a thin adapter.

```ts
// ---------- Money ----------

type CurrencyCode = "USD" | "EUR" | "GBP" | "JPY";

/** Amount is in minor units (cents), never a float. */
interface Money {
  amount: number;
  currency: CurrencyCode;
}

// ---------- Images ----------

interface ProductImage {
  url: string;
  alt: string;          // required: never render an image without alt text
  width: number;
  height: number;       // lets you reserve space and avoid layout shift
  thumbnailUrl?: string;
}

// ---------- Rich description ----------

/** Inline formatting inside a block of text. */
interface TextSpan {
  text: string;
  bold?: boolean;
  italic?: boolean;
  href?: string;
}

interface ParagraphBlock {
  type: "paragraph";
  content: TextSpan[];
}

interface HeadingBlock {
  type: "heading";
  level: 2 | 3 | 4;
  content: TextSpan[];
}

interface ListBlock {
  type: "list";
  ordered: boolean;
  items: TextSpan[][];
}

interface ImageBlock {
  type: "image";
  image: ProductImage;
  caption?: string;
}

interface SpecsBlock {
  type: "specs";
  rows: { label: string; value: string }[];
}

type DescriptionBlock =
  | ParagraphBlock
  | HeadingBlock
  | ListBlock
  | ImageBlock
  | SpecsBlock;

// ---------- Product ----------

interface Product {
  id: string;
  slug: string;
  name: string;
  /** Current selling price. */
  price: Money;
  /** Original price, for strikethrough display. */
  compareAtPrice?: Money;
  /** First entry is the primary image. */
  images: ProductImage[];
  description: DescriptionBlock[];
  inStock: boolean;
}

type ProductPageData = {
  product: Product;
  /** e.g. "Home / Shoes / Runner" */
  breadcrumbs: { label: string; href: string }[];
};
```

**Why these choices**

- **`Money` as minor units + currency code.** `19.99` as a float breaks arithmetic (`0.1 + 0.2`), and a bare number is meaningless without knowing the currency. Integers in cents plus an explicit code is the standard fix.
- **Blocks instead of HTML or Markdown.** Storing the description as a discriminated union means the renderer is a `switch` over `type` with exhaustiveness checking — no `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`, no XSS surface, no runtime Markdown parser, and it renders identically on web and native. A typo in a block type is a compile error, not a blank section.
- **`TextSpan` for inline formatting.** Paragraph text is rarely plain — you need bold runs and inline links. Modeling those as spans keeps it type-safe without dropping to HTML.
- **`alt` and `width`/`height` are required.** Accessibility and layout shift are the two things most often forgotten when image types are written as `string[]`.

**Two options worth considering**

Guarantee at least one image with a non-empty tuple:

```ts
images: [ProductImage, ...ProductImage[]];
```

And for the renderer, an exhaustiveness guard so adding a block type fails the build:

```ts
function assertNever(x: never): never {
  throw new Error(`Unhandled block: ${JSON.stringify(x)}`);
}
```

If your CMS already emits sanitized HTML, a `type: "html"; html: string` block is a pragmatic escape hatch — just make sure the sanitization happens server-side, not at render time.

# TypeScript Types for a Product Page

Here's a well-structured set of types. The key design decision is modeling the **long description as a discriminated union of content blocks** rather than a raw HTML string — this keeps it type-safe, renderable, and safe from XSS.

```ts
// ---------- Primitives ----------

/** ISO 4217 currency code, e.g. "USD", "EUR". */
export type CurrencyCode = string;

/** Price in the smallest currency unit (e.g. cents) to avoid float issues. */
export interface Money {
  amount: number;        // 1999 === $19.99
  currency: CurrencyCode;
}

// ---------- Images ----------

export interface ProductImage {
  id: string;
  url: string;
  alt: string;
  width: number;
  height: number;
  /** Optional blurhash / LQIP for progressive loading. */
  placeholder?: string;
}

// ---------- Rich description blocks ----------

export interface ParagraphBlock {
  type: "paragraph";
  text: string;
}

export interface HeadingBlock {
  type: "heading";
  level: 2 | 3 | 4;
  text: string;
}

export interface ListBlock {
  type: "list";
  ordered: boolean;
  items: string[];
}

export interface ImageBlock {
  type: "image";
  image: ProductImage;
  caption?: string;
}

export interface QuoteBlock {
  type: "quote";
  text: string;
  attribution?: string;
}

export interface CodeBlock {
  type: "code";
  language: string;
  code: string;
}

export interface TableBlock {
  type: "table";
  headers: string[];
  rows: string[][];
}

export type DescriptionBlock =
  | ParagraphBlock
  | HeadingBlock
  | ListBlock
  | ImageBlock
  | QuoteBlock
  | CodeBlock
  | TableBlock;

// ---------- Product ----------

export interface Product {
  id: string;
  slug: string;
  name: string;
  /** Short tagline shown near the title. */
  tagline?: string;
  price: Money;
  /** Optional original price for showing a discount. */
  compareAtPrice?: Money;
  images: ProductImage[];
  /** Long-form, formatted description. */
  description: DescriptionBlock[];
  /** Optional plain-text summary for SEO / previews. */
  descriptionPlain?: string;
  inStock: boolean;
  tags?: string[];
  updatedAt: string; // ISO 8601
}
```

## Why this shape?

- **`Money` as `{ amount, currency }`** — storing cents as an integer avoids floating-point rounding bugs (`0.1 + 0.2 !== 0.3`), and keeping the currency alongside the amount prevents mixing currencies.
- **Discriminated union for `description`** — each block has a `type` field, so a renderer can `switch (block.type)` and TypeScript will narrow the shape automatically. This is far safer than `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` with a raw HTML string.
- **`ProductImage` reused** in both the gallery and inline description images — one source of truth for dimensions/alt text.
- **`descriptionPlain`** — useful for `<meta name="description">`, search indexing, or card previews without parsing blocks.

## Example usage

```ts
const product: Product = {
  id: "p_123",
  slug: "ceramic-mug",
  name: "Handmade Ceramic Mug",
  tagline: "Wheel-thrown, glazed in matte sage",
  price: { amount: 2400, currency: "USD" },
  compareAtPrice: { amount: 3200, currency: "USD" },
  images: [
    {
      id: "img_1",
      url: "/mug-front.jpg",
      alt: "Front view of the sage ceramic mug",
      width: 1200,
      height: 1200,
    },
  ],
  description: [
    { type: "paragraph", text: "Thrown on the wheel in small batches." },
    { type: "heading", level: 3, text: "Details" },
    {
      type: "list",
      ordered: false,
      items: ["12 oz capacity", "Dishwasher safe", "Food-safe glaze"],
    },
    {
      type: "image",
      image: {
        id: "img_2",
        url: "/mug-detail.jpg",
        alt: "Close-up of the glaze",
        width: 800,
        height: 800,
      },
      caption: "Matte sage glaze with a subtle speckle.",
    },
  ],
  inStock: true,
  updatedAt: "2025-01-15T10:00:00Z",
};
```

## Rendering the blocks

A small renderer that benefits from the discriminated union:

```tsx
function renderBlock(block: DescriptionBlock) {
  switch (block.type) {
    case "paragraph":
      return <p>{block.text}</p>;
    case "heading": {
      const Tag = `h${block.level}` as const;
      return <Tag>{block.text}</Tag>;
    }
    case "list":
      return block.ordered
        ? <ol>{block.items.map((i) => <li key={i}>{i}</li>)}</ol>
        : <ul>{block.items.map((i) => <li key={i}>{i}</li>)}</ul>;
    case "image":
      return (
        <figure>
          <img src={block.image.url} alt={block.image.alt} />
          {block.caption && <figcaption>{block.caption}</figcaption>}
        </figure>
      );
    case "quote":
      return (
        <blockquote>
          {block.text}
          {block.attribution && <cite>— {block.attribution}</cite>}
        </blockquote>
      );
    case "code":
      return <pre data-lang={block.language}><code>{block.code}</code></pre>;
    case "table":
      return (
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>{block.headers.map((h) => <th key={h}>{h}</th>)}</tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {block.rows.map((row, i) => (
              <tr key={i}>{row.map((cell, j) => <td key={j}>{cell}</td>)}</tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      );
  }
}
```

If you'd rather store the description as **Markdown** or **HTML** instead of blocks, swap `description: DescriptionBlock[]` for `description: string` — but you lose the type-level guarantees and will need a sanitizer (e.g. DOMPurify) at render time.

Here's a set of TypeScript types for a product page, structured so the long description stays renderable without `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`.

```ts
// ---------- Shared primitives ----------

/** Money is stored in minor units (cents, pence, yen) as an integer to avoid float drift. */
export interface Money {
  /** Integer minor units, e.g. 1999 for $19.99 */
  amount: number;
  currency: CurrencyCode;
}

export type CurrencyCode = 'USD' | 'EUR' | 'GBP' | 'JPY' | 'CAD';

// ---------- Images ----------

export interface ProductImage {
  id: string;
  url: string;
  /** Required: alt text is not optional for a product page. */
  alt: string;
  width: number;
  height: number;
  /** Optional placeholder for progressive loading. */
  blurhash?: string;
}

// ---------- Rich description ----------

/** Inline styling applied to a run of text. Links are marks so they can nest inside any inline flow. */
export type TextMark =
  | { type: 'bold' }
  | { type: 'italic' }
  | { type: 'underline' }
  | { type: 'strikethrough' }
  | { type: 'code' }
  | { type: 'link'; href: string };

export interface TextNode {
  type: 'text';
  text: string;
  marks?: TextMark[];
}

export interface LineBreakNode {
  type: 'lineBreak';
}

export type InlineNode = TextNode | LineBreakNode;

// ---------- Block nodes ----------

export interface ParagraphBlock {
  type: 'paragraph';
  children: InlineNode[];
}

export interface HeadingBlock {
  type: 'heading';
  /** Keep the semantic level explicit so the page keeps a valid heading outline. */
  level: 2 | 3 | 4;
  children: InlineNode[];
}

export interface ListBlock {
  type: 'list';
  style: 'ordered' | 'unordered';
  items: InlineNode[][];
}

export interface QuoteBlock {
  type: 'quote';
  children: InlineNode[];
  attribution?: string;
}

export interface ImageBlock {
  type: 'image';
  image: ProductImage;
  caption?: string;
}

export interface DividerBlock {
  type: 'divider';
}

/** Discriminated on `type`, so a switch over it is exhaustive. */
export type DescriptionBlock =
  | ParagraphBlock
  | HeadingBlock
  | ListBlock
  | QuoteBlock
  | ImageBlock
  | DividerBlock;

// ---------- The page ----------

export interface Product {
  id: string;
  /** URL-safe identifier, e.g. "linen-oxford-shirt" */
  slug: string;
  name: string;
  price: Money;
  /** Original price, present only when the item is discounted. */
  compareAtPrice?: Money;
  /** Ordered gallery. `images[0]` is the primary/hero image. */
  images: ProductImage[];
  /** Plain-text summary for cards, meta description, and search results. */
  shortDescription: string;
  /** Structured rich text; render it yourself instead of injecting HTML. */
  description: DescriptionBlock[];
}

export interface ProductPageData {
  product: Product;
  breadcrumbs: Array<{ label: string; href: string }>;
  /** ISO 8601 */
  updatedAt: string;
}
```

A few things worth calling out:

**Why blocks instead of an HTML string.** A `description: string` typed as HTML forces you to `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` and gives you zero guarantees about what's inside. The block union means the renderer is a plain exhaustive switch, sanitization happens at the boundary, and you can restyle or re-target the same content to email, PDF, or a native app.

**Why `amount` is an integer.** `0.1 + 0.2 !== 0.3` is a real bug in checkout totals. Store 1999, format at the edge. If you need fractional-unit currencies later, add a `scale` field rather than switching to floats.

**Why links are marks.** Keeping `link` in `TextMark` rather than as its own node type means a link can live inside a paragraph, heading, list item, or quote without duplicating four node types.

**Exhaustiveness helper.** This pairs well with the union:

```ts
function assertNever(x: never): never {
  throw new Error(`Unhandled block: ${JSON.stringify(x)}`);
}
```

If you're validating at runtime (API responses, CMS payloads), mirror this with Zod and derive the types via `z.infer` — that way the types can't drift from what actually gets parsed.

```ts
// ---------- Primitives ----------

/** ISO 4217 currency code, e.g. "USD", "EUR". */
export type CurrencyCode = string;

/** Minor-unit amount (e.g. cents) to avoid float rounding issues. */
export type Money = {
  amount: number;
  currency: CurrencyCode;
};

// ---------- Images ----------

export type ProductImage = {
  id: string;
  url: string;
  alt: string;
  width: number;
  height: number;
  /** Optional blurhash / base64 LQIP for progressive loading. */
  placeholder?: string;
};

// ---------- Rich description ----------

/**
 * Inline formatting spans. `text` is the raw string; the marks describe
 * how to render it. Keep this flat and let the renderer compose them.
 */
export type TextSpan = {
  type: "text";
  text: string;
  marks?: Array<"bold" | "italic" | "underline" | "code" | "strike">;
  /** Optional link target when the span is a hyperlink. */
  href?: string;
};

export type LinkSpan = {
  type: "link";
  href: string;
  children: TextSpan[];
};

export type InlineNode = TextSpan | LinkSpan;

/** Block-level nodes for the long description. */
export type DescriptionBlock =
  | { type: "paragraph"; children: InlineNode[] }
  | { type: "heading"; level: 2 | 3 | 4; children: InlineNode[] }
  | { type: "list"; style: "bullet" | "number"; items: InlineNode[][] }
  | { type: "quote"; children: InlineNode[] }
  | { type: "code"; language?: string; code: string }
  | { type: "image"; image: ProductImage; caption?: string }
  | { type: "divider" };

// ---------- Product ----------

export type ProductAvailability =
  | { status: "in_stock"; quantity: number }
  | { status: "low_stock"; quantity: number }
  | { status: "out_of_stock" }
  | { status: "preorder"; releaseDate: string };

export type ProductPage = {
  id: string;
  slug: string;
  name: string;
  /** Short tagline shown under the name. */
  tagline?: string;
  price: Money;
  /** Optional original price for showing a strikethrough. */
  compareAtPrice?: Money;
  images: ProductImage[];
  /** Structured rich text; render with a block renderer. */
  description: DescriptionBlock[];
  availability: ProductAvailability;
  /** Free-form tags for filtering / breadcrumbs. */
  tags?: string[];
  updatedAt: string;
};
```

A few notes on the choices:

- **`Money` as minor units** — storing `1999` instead of `19.99` avoids floating-point drift when you start doing math (discounts, tax, totals).
- **Block-based description** — instead of a raw HTML string, the description is an array of typed blocks. This keeps rendering safe (no `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`), makes it easy to style each block type, and matches what most headless CMSs (Sanity Portable Text, Contentful Rich Text, Notion) already emit.
- **`InlineNode` union** — text spans carry their own marks, so `**bold** and *italic*` in the same paragraph is just two spans. Links are a separate node so they can nest spans.
- **`availability` as a discriminated union** — the `status` field narrows the shape, so `if (p.availability.status === "low_stock")` gives you `quantity` for free.

If you'd rather keep it simpler and your CMS already returns HTML, you can swap `description: DescriptionBlock[]` for `description: string` and sanitize on render — but you lose type safety on the formatting.

