If you'd rather store the description as **Markdown** or **HTML**, swap `description: RichText` for `description: string` — but you'll lose the type-level guarantees and need to sanitize at render time.

```ts
// ---------- Primitives ----------

/** ISO 4217 currency code, e.g. "USD", "EUR". */
export type CurrencyCode = string;

/** Minor-unit amount (e.g. cents) to avoid float rounding issues. */
export type Money = {
  amount: number;
  currency: CurrencyCode;
};

// ---------- Images ----------

export type ProductImage = {
  id: string;
  url: string;
  alt: string;
  width: number;
  height: number;
  /** Optional blurhash / base64 LQIP for progressive loading. */
  placeholder?: string;
};

// ---------- Rich description ----------

/**
 * Inline formatting spans. `text` is the raw string; the marks describe
 * how to render it. Keep this flat and let the renderer compose them.
 */
export type TextSpan = {
  type: "text";
  text: string;
  marks?: Array<"bold" | "italic" | "underline" | "code" | "strike">;
  /** Optional link target when the span is a hyperlink. */
  href?: string;
};

export type LinkSpan = {
  type: "link";
  href: string;
  children: TextSpan[];
};

export type InlineNode = TextSpan | LinkSpan;

/** Block-level nodes for the long description. */
export type DescriptionBlock =
  | { type: "paragraph"; children: InlineNode[] }
  | { type: "heading"; level: 2 | 3 | 4; children: InlineNode[] }
  | { type: "list"; style: "bullet" | "number"; items: InlineNode[][] }
  | { type: "quote"; children: InlineNode[] }
  | { type: "code"; language?: string; code: string }
  | { type: "image"; image: ProductImage; caption?: string }
  | { type: "divider" };

// ---------- Product ----------

export type ProductAvailability =
  | { status: "in_stock"; quantity: number }
  | { status: "low_stock"; quantity: number }
  | { status: "out_of_stock" }
  | { status: "preorder"; releaseDate: string };

export type ProductPage = {
  id: string;
  slug: string;
  name: string;
  /** Short tagline shown under the name. */
  tagline?: string;
  price: Money;
  /** Optional original price for showing a strikethrough. */
  compareAtPrice?: Money;
  images: ProductImage[];
  /** Structured rich text; render with a block renderer. */
  description: DescriptionBlock[];
  availability: ProductAvailability;
  /** Free-form tags for filtering / breadcrumbs. */
  tags?: string[];
  updatedAt: string;
};
```

A few notes on the choices:

- **`Money` as minor units** — storing `1999` instead of `19.99` avoids floating-point drift when you start doing math (discounts, tax, totals).
- **Block-based description** — instead of a raw HTML string, the description is an array of typed blocks. This keeps rendering safe (no `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`), makes it easy to style each block type, and matches what most headless CMSs (Sanity Portable Text, Contentful Rich Text, Notion) already emit.
- **`InlineNode` union** — text spans carry their own marks, so `**bold** and *italic*` in the same paragraph is just two spans. Links are a separate node so they can nest spans.
- **`availability` as a discriminated union** — the `status` field narrows the shape, so `if (p.availability.status === "low_stock")` gives you `quantity` for free.

If you'd rather keep it simpler and your CMS already returns HTML, you can swap `description: DescriptionBlock[]` for `description: string` and sanitize on render — but you lose type safety on the formatting.

# TypeScript Types for a Product Page

Here's a well-structured set of types. The key design decision is modeling the "long description with formatting" as a **discriminated union of content blocks** rather than a raw HTML string — this keeps it type-safe, renderable across platforms (web, native, email), and safe from XSS.

```ts
// ---------- Primitives ----------

/** ISO 4217 currency code, e.g. "USD", "EUR". */
export type CurrencyCode = string;

/** A monetary amount. Stored in minor units (cents) to avoid float errors. */
export interface Money {
  /** Amount in the smallest currency unit, e.g. 1999 = $19.99 */
  amount: number;
  currency: CurrencyCode;
}

// ---------- Images ----------

export interface ProductImage {
  /** Stable identifier, useful as a React key. */
  id: string;
  /** Full-size image URL. */
  url: string;
  /** Optional responsive variants. */
  srcSet?: string;
  /** Alt text for accessibility. Required for a11y. */
  alt: string;
  /** Intrinsic dimensions, helps prevent layout shift. */
  width?: number;
  height?: number;
  /** Optional blurhash / LQIP placeholder. */
  placeholder?: string;
}

// ---------- Rich description ----------

/** Inline text with optional styling. */
export interface TextSpan {
  type: "text";
  text: string;
  bold?: boolean;
  italic?: boolean;
  code?: boolean;
  /** Optional link target. */
  href?: string;
}

/** A paragraph is a sequence of inline spans. */
export interface ParagraphBlock {
  type: "paragraph";
  children: TextSpan[];
}

export interface HeadingBlock {
  type: "heading";
  level: 2 | 3 | 4;
  children: TextSpan[];
}

export interface ListBlock {
  type: "list";
  ordered: boolean;
  items: TextSpan[][];
}

export interface ImageBlock {
  type: "image";
  image: ProductImage;
  caption?: string;
}

export interface QuoteBlock {
  type: "quote";
  children: TextSpan[];
  attribution?: string;
}

export interface DividerBlock {
  type: "divider";
}

/** Discriminated union — exhaustive rendering is enforced by the compiler. */
export type DescriptionBlock =
  | ParagraphBlock
  | HeadingBlock
  | ListBlock
  | ImageBlock
  | QuoteBlock
  | DividerBlock;

export interface RichDescription {
  /** Plain-text fallback for SEO, previews, and search. */
  plainText: string;
  /** Structured content for rendering. */
  blocks: DescriptionBlock[];
}

// ---------- Product ----------

export interface Product {
  id: string;
  /** URL-safe identifier, e.g. "classic-cotton-tee". */
  slug: string;
  name: string;
  /** Short tagline shown near the title. */
  tagline?: string;
  price: Money;
  /** Optional original price for showing a strikethrough. */
  compareAtPrice?: Money;
  /** Primary image is `images[0]`; keep at least one. */
  images: [ProductImage, ...ProductImage[]];
  description: RichDescription;
  /** Optional structured attributes, e.g. { Material: "Cotton" }. */
  attributes?: Record<string, string>;
  inStock: boolean;
}
```

## Why this shape

- **`Money` as `{ amount, currency }`** — avoids the classic `price: number` bug where you can't tell dollars from cents, and keeps currency explicit for i18n.
- **`images` as a non-empty tuple** — the type system guarantees at least one image, so `product.images[0]` is always safe.
- **`DescriptionBlock` discriminated union** — you can render with an exhaustive `switch` and the compiler will flag any unhandled block type. It also lets you render the same content to web, React Native, or email without parsing HTML.
- **`plainText` alongside `blocks`** — you almost always need a plain-text version for `<meta description>`, search indexing, or previews. Storing it avoids re-deriving it at runtime.

## Example usage

```ts
