## Alternatives to the rich-text AST

If a structured AST is too heavy for your stack, swap `description` for one of these — both still type-check cleanly:

```ts
// Markdown: simple, but you trust the source.
interface MarkdownDescription { format: 'markdown'; value: string; }

// HTML: render via a sanitizer, never raw innerHTML.
interface HtmlDescription { format: 'html'; value: string; }

type Description = RichTextDocument | MarkdownDescription | HtmlDescription;
```

## A few things worth doing in practice

- **`images` as a tuple** (`[ProductImage, ...ProductImage[]]`) if a product must always have at least one — the type then enforces what your UI already assumes.
- **Currency-aware rendering.** Use `Intl.NumberFormat(currency, { style: 'currency' })` on `amount / 100`, not string math.
- **Derive `plainText` at build time** from the blocks so search and `meta description` never drift from the visible copy.

Here's a set of types built around two ideas: **money as integer minor units** (no float rounding) and **description as a structured AST** rather than an HTML string (safe to render, easy to style, portable across CMSs).

```ts
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* Money                                                               */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

type CurrencyCode = 'USD' | 'EUR' | 'GBP' | 'JPY' | (string & {});

interface Money {
  /** Smallest currency unit — cents, pence, yen. Never a float. */
  amount: number;
  currency: CurrencyCode;
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* Images                                                              */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

interface ProductImage {
  id: string;
  url: string;
  /** Required — decorative images should use alt="" explicitly. */
  alt: string;
  width: number;
  height: number;
  /** Tiny base64 placeholder to avoid layout shift / pop-in. */
  blurDataUrl?: string;
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* Rich description                                                    */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

type TextMark = 'bold' | 'italic' | 'underline' | 'strikethrough' | 'code';

type InlineNode =
  | { type: 'text'; text: string; marks?: TextMark[] }
  | { type: 'link'; href: string; children: InlineNode[] }
  | { type: 'lineBreak' };

interface ListItem {
  /** Blocks, not just inlines, so list items can hold nested lists. */
  children: BlockNode[];
}

type BlockNode =
  | { type: 'paragraph'; children: InlineNode[] }
  | { type: 'heading'; level: 2 | 3 | 4; children: InlineNode[] }
  | { type: 'list'; style: 'bullet' | 'numbered'; items: ListItem[] }
  | { type: 'quote'; children: InlineNode[]; cite?: string }
  | { type: 'code'; language?: string; code: string }
  | { type: 'image'; image: ProductImage; caption?: InlineNode[] }
  | { type: 'divider' };

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* Product page                                                        */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

interface ProductPage {
  id: string;
  /** URL segment, e.g. "linen-oxford-shirt". */
  slug: string;

  name: string;
  /** One-liner for cards, meta tags, and the buy box. */
  tagline?: string;

  price: Money;
  /** Present only when the item is discounted; render as struck-through. */
  compareAtPrice?: Money;

  images: ProductImage[];
  /** Index into `images` for the hero shot. Defaults to 0. */
  primaryImageIndex?: number;

  /** The long-form copy. Render block-by-block. */
  description: BlockNode[];
}
```

### Rendering the description

The discriminated unions make the renderer exhaustive — TypeScript will flag any block type you forget:

```tsx
function Block({ node }: { node: BlockNode }) {
  switch (node.type) {
    case 'paragraph':
      return <p><Inline nodes={node.children} /></p>;
    case 'heading': {
      const Tag = `h${node.level}` as const;
      return <Tag><Inline nodes={node.children} /></Tag>;
    }
    case 'list': {
      const Tag = node.style === 'bullet' ? 'ul' : 'ol';
      return (
        <Tag>
          {node.items.map((item, i) => (
            <li key={i}>{item.children.map((b, j) => <Block key={j} node={b} />)}</li>
          ))}
        </Tag>
      );
    }
    // ... the rest
  }
}
```

### Why not just `description: string`?

- **HTML string** — forces `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`, which is an XSS hole the moment the CMS is writable by anyone you don't fully trust, and it leaks styling concerns into content.
- **Markdown string** — safer, but you re-parse on every render and lose the ability to attach typed metadata (image dimensions, link targets, captions).
- **Structured AST** — validated once at the data boundary (e.g. with Zod), then rendered with plain components. Same shape works for a web page, a native app, and an email template.

If your content really is just a couple of paragraphs, `description: string` is fine — but the moment you need a bulleted spec list and an inline image, you'll be retrofitting this.

### A few things worth adding later

- `inStock: boolean` or `inventory: { status: 'in_stock' | 'low' | 'out_of_stock'; quantity?: number }`
- `variants: ProductVariant[]` (size/color), each with its own `price` and `images`
- `sku`, `brand`, `rating: { average: number; count: number }`
- `seo: { title: string; description: string; ogImage?: ProductImage }`

```ts
