```

A few notes on the design choices:

- **`Money` uses minor units** (`amount: 1234` = `$12.34`). This avoids the classic `0.1 + 0.2` float bug when summing totals, and makes currency explicit rather than implied.
- **Description is a structured document, not HTML or Markdown.** A discriminated union on `type` gives you exhaustive `switch` handling in the renderer, and it's directly serializable from most headless CMSs (Sanity, Contentful, Strapi). If your CMS only gives you HTML or Markdown strings, swap `RichTextDocument` for `string` — but you lose type safety over the content.
- **`RichTextSpan` separates text from marks** rather than nesting, which keeps the tree shallow and easy to render.
- **`ProductVariant`** is optional but included because most real product pages need size/color options, and retrofitting it later is painful.
- **`ProductPage` wraps `Product`** so you can add page-level concerns (breadcrumbs, related items, SEO metadata) without polluting the product entity itself.

If you'd rather keep it minimal, the core is just:

```ts
interface Product {
  name: string;
  price: Money;
  images: ProductImage[];
  description: RichTextDocument;
}
```

Here's a set of TypeScript types for a product page with a name, price, images, and a formatted long description.
```ts
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *
 * Small helpers
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

/** Nominal typing so a ProductId can't be passed where a slug is expected. */
type Brand<T, B extends string> = T & { readonly __brand: B };

export type ProductId = Brand<string, 'ProductId'>;

export type NonEmptyArray<T> = readonly [T, ...T[]];

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *
 * Money & price
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

export interface Money {
  /** Integer amount in the currency's minor unit (1999 = $19.99). */
  readonly amount: number;
  /** ISO 4217 code, e.g. "USD", "EUR", "JPY". */
  readonly currency: string;
}

export interface Price {
  /** What the customer pays today. */
  readonly current: Money;
  /** Pre-discount price. Present only when the item is on sale. */
  readonly compareAt?: Money;
  /** Unit qualifier for non-one-time pricing, e.g. "per month". */
  readonly unit?: string;
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *
 * Images
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

export interface ProductImage {
  readonly id: string;
  readonly url: string;
  /** Required: every product image needs meaningful alt text. */
  readonly alt: string;
  /** Intrinsic dimensions, so the layout can reserve space (no CLS). */
  readonly width: number;
  readonly height: number;
  /** Optional LQIP / blurhash shown while the full image loads. */
  readonly placeholder?: string;
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *
 * Rich text (long description)
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

export type TextMark =
  | { readonly type: 'bold' }
  | { readonly type: 'italic' }
  | { readonly type: 'underline' }
  | { readonly type: 'strikethrough' }
  | { readonly type: 'code' }
  | { readonly type: 'link'; readonly href: string; readonly external?: boolean };

export interface TextSpan {
  readonly text: string;
  /** Omitted or empty = plain text. */
  readonly marks?: readonly TextMark[];
}

export interface ListItem {
  readonly children: readonly TextSpan[];
  /** Nested items, one level deeper. */
  readonly items?: readonly ListItem[];
}

/** A block of content. Discriminated on `type`. */
export type RichTextBlock =
  | { readonly type: 'paragraph'; readonly children: readonly TextSpan[] }
  | {
      readonly type: 'heading';
      readonly level: 2 | 3 | 4; // h1 is the product name, not content
      readonly children: readonly TextSpan[];
    }
  | { readonly type: 'list'; readonly ordered: boolean; readonly items: readonly ListItem[] }
  | { readonly type: 'quote'; readonly children: readonly TextSpan[]; readonly cite?: string }
  | { readonly type: 'code'; readonly code: string; readonly language?: string }
  | { readonly type: 'image'; readonly image: ProductImage; readonly caption?: string }
  | { readonly type: 'divider' };

export interface RichTextDocument {
  readonly blocks: readonly RichTextBlock[];
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *
 * Product page
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

export interface ProductPage {
  readonly id: ProductId;
  /** URL segment, e.g. "merino-wool-runner". */
  readonly slug: string;
  /** Display name, rendered as the page's h1. */
  readonly name: string;
  /** Optional short hook shown above the price. */
  readonly tagline?: string;
  /** The long, formatted description. */
  readonly description: RichTextDocument;
  readonly price: Price;
  /** At least one image; the first is treated as the primary. */
  readonly images: NonEmptyArray<ProductImage>;
  readonly inStock?: boolean;
}
```
### Structure and Type Design

This set of types keeps the product data safe and predictable.

- **Price**: The `Money` type stores integer amounts in minor units (like cents) to avoid floating-point rounding issues. `Price` wraps this with optional sale price and unit information.
- **Images**: `ProductImage` requires meaningful `alt` text and intrinsic width/height for layout stability. `NonEmptyArray` guarantees at least one image.
- **Rich text**: The description uses a block-based format with paragraphs, headings, lists, quotes, code blocks, and inline images. Text spans can carry formatting marks like bold, links, or code.
- **Discriminated unions**: Every block has a `type` field, so you can safely switch on it to render content.
- **Immutability**: All fields are marked `readonly`, and branded `ProductId` prevents accidental ID mix-ups.
---

**Optimization Tip:** You can adjust the `RichTextBlock` union if your editor outputs different block types, and add currency or locale handling for `Money` if needed.

# Product Page TypeScript Types

Here's a well-structured set of types. The key design decision is how to model the "long description with formatting" — I've used a **block-based rich text model** (similar to Portable Text / Sanity / Notion) because it's type-safe, renderer-agnostic, and avoids the pitfalls of raw HTML or Markdown strings.

```ts
// ---------- Rich text ----------

/** Inline formatting spans within a text block. */
export type TextSpan = {
  text: string;
  marks?: Array<"bold" | "italic" | "underline" | "code" | "strikethrough">;
  /** Internal link (e.g. to another product) or external URL. */
  link?: { href: string; external?: boolean };
};

export type TextBlock = {
  _type: "text";
  style?: "normal" | "h2" | "h3" | "h4" | "blockquote";
  spans: TextSpan[];
};

export type ListBlock = {
  _type: "list";
  ordered: boolean;
  items: TextSpan[][];
};

export type ImageBlock = {
  _type: "image";
  image: ProductImage;
  caption?: string;
};

export type DescriptionBlock = TextBlock | ListBlock | ImageBlock;

// ---------- Media ----------

export type ProductImage = {
  id: string;
  url: string;
  alt: string;
  width: number;
  height: number;
  /** Optional focal point (0–1) for smart cropping. */
  focalPoint?: { x: number; y: number };
};

// ---------- Price ----------

export type Money = {
  /** Minor units (e.g. cents) to avoid float rounding issues. */
  amount: number;
  /** ISO 4217 code, e.g. "USD", "EUR". */
  currency: string;
};

export type Price = {
  current: Money;
  /** Original price if the item is on sale. */
  compareAt?: Money;
  /** Percentage 0–100, precomputed for display convenience. */
  discountPercent?: number;
};

// ---------- Product ----------

export type ProductAvailability =
  | { status: "in_stock"; quantity: number }
  | { status: "low_stock"; quantity: number }
  | { status: "out_of_stock"; restockDate?: string }
  | { status: "preorder"; releaseDate: string };

export type ProductPage = {
  id: string;
  slug: string;
  name: string;
  /** Short tagline shown above the fold. */
  tagline?: string;
  price: Price;
  images: ProductImage[];
  /** Long-form, formatted description. */
  description: DescriptionBlock[];
  availability: ProductAvailability;
  /** Optional structured specs for a details table. */
  specs?: Array<{ label: string; value: string }>;
  updatedAt: string; // ISO 8601
};
```

## Why this shape?

- **`Money.amount` in minor units** — avoids `0.1 + 0.2` float bugs; format at the edge.
- **Block-based description** — the renderer (React, native, email) decides how to render. No `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`, no Markdown-parsing at runtime, and every node is exhaustively type-checked.
- **Discriminated unions** (`_type`, `status`) — TypeScript narrows automatically in `switch`/`if` blocks.
- **`ProductImage` reused** in both the gallery and inline description images.

## Example renderer

```tsx
function renderBlock(block: DescriptionBlock, key: number) {
  switch (block._type) {
    case "text": {
      const Tag = block.style ?? "normal";
      return <Tag key={key}>{block.spans.map(renderSpan)}</Tag>;
    }
    case "list": {
      const Tag = block.ordered ? "ol" : "ul";
      return (
        <Tag key={key}>
          {block.items.map((item, i) => <li key={i}>{item.map(renderSpan)}</li>)}
        </Tag>
      );
    }
    case "image":
      return <figure key={key}><img {...block.image} /><figcaption>{block.caption}</figcaption></figure>;
  }
}
```

If you'd rather store the description as **Markdown** (`description: string`) or **HTML**, that's fine too — but you lose the ability to validate structure at the type level and end up parsing/trusting strings at render time.

```ts
// ---------- Shared primitives ----------

/** ISO 4217 currency code, e.g. "USD", "EUR". */
type CurrencyCode = string;

/**
 * Money is stored in minor units (cents, pence, etc.) as an integer
 * to avoid floating-point rounding errors.
 */
interface Money {
  amount: number; // e.g. 1999 === $19.99
  currency: CurrencyCode;
}

/** A price that may be discounted from a previous value. */
interface Price {
  current: Money;
  /** Present only when the item is on sale. */
  original?: Money;
  /** Percentage off, derived or supplied by the backend (0–100). */
  discountPercent?: number;
}

// ---------- Images ----------

interface ProductImage {
  id: string;
  url: string;
  /** Required for accessibility; describe the image, don't repeat the name. */
  alt: string;
  width?: number;
  height?: number;
  /** Optional low-res placeholder for progressive loading. */
  blurDataUrl?: string;
}

// ---------- Formatted description ----------

/**
 * The description is modeled as an array of blocks rather than an HTML
 * string, so it stays type-safe, sanitized, and renderable to any target.
 */
type InlineNode =
  | { type: "text"; value: string }
  | { type: "strong"; children: InlineNode[] }
  | { type: "em"; children: InlineNode[] }
  | { type: "code"; value: string }
  | { type: "link"; href: string; children: InlineNode[] };

type DescriptionBlock =
  | { type: "heading"; level: 2 | 3 | 4; children: InlineNode[] }
  | { type: "paragraph"; children: InlineNode[] }
  | { type: "list"; ordered: boolean; items: InlineNode[][] }
  | { type: "image"; image: ProductImage; caption?: string }
  | { type: "quote"; children: InlineNode[]; attribution?: string }
  | { type: "divider" };

interface RichDescription {
  blocks: DescriptionBlock[];
}

// ---------- Product ----------

interface Product {
  id: string;
  /** URL-safe identifier, e.g. "linen-oxford-shirt". */
  slug: string;
  name: string;
  /** Short one-liner shown under the name. */
  tagline?: string;
  price: Price;
  images: ProductImage[];
  /** At least one image is expected; the first is the primary. */
  description: RichDescription;
  /** Optional structured specs, rendered as a table. */
  specs?: Record<string, string>;
  inStock: boolean;
}
```

A few notes on the choices:

- **`Money.amount` as an integer** — never store prices as floats. `1999` + `"USD"` beats `19.99` every time.
- **Description as blocks, not HTML** — a discriminated union gives you exhaustive `switch` rendering, keeps user content safe from XSS, and lets you render to React, React Native, email, or plain text from the same data.
- **`InlineNode` is recursive** — this lets you nest `**bold *and italic* text**` without special-casing.
- **`images` is required and non-empty by convention** — if you want to enforce it at the type level, use `[ProductImage, ...ProductImage[]]`.

If your backend already returns Markdown or HTML, you can swap `RichDescription` for `{ format: "markdown" | "html"; content: string }` and parse it at the edge — but the block model is worth the extra work if you control the CMS.

Here's a type system for a product page. The interesting part is the description — I model it as a discriminated union of content blocks (like a mini rich-text format) so it's portable, safe to render, and doesn't depend on HTML.

