```

### Why this shape

- **Discriminated unions over HTML strings.** `DescriptionBlock` and `InlineNode` are tagged by `type`, so a renderer can switch exhaustively and TypeScript will flag any unhandled case. You also avoid `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` and XSS risk from user- or CMS-authored content.
- **Money as minor units.** `amount: 1200` + `currency: "USD"` avoids floating-point drift (`0.1 + 0.2` problems) and keeps the currency explicit for locale-aware formatting.
- **Nesting is intentional.** `InlineNode` is recursive, so bold-inside-link-inside-italic works naturally without a separate type per combination.

### If you'd rather keep it simpler

Two common alternatives for the description field:

```ts
// Markdown (easy to author, render with a library)
description: string;

// Or, if you want a hybrid:
description: { format: 'markdown' | 'blocks'; value: string | DescriptionBlock[] };
```

Markdown is fine if authors are technical and you trust the source. Blocks are worth the extra ceremony when the content comes from a CMS or non-technical editors, because you control exactly what's renderable.

### Rendering hint

```tsx
function renderBlock(block: DescriptionBlock, key: number) {
  switch (block.type) {
    case 'paragraph':
      return <p key={key}>{block.content.map(renderInline)}</p>;
    case 'heading':
      return React.createElement(`h${block.level}`, { key }, block.content.map(renderInline));
    case 'list': {
      const Tag = block.ordered ? 'ol' : 'ul';
      return <Tag key={key}>{block.items.map((item, i) => <li key={i}>{item.map(renderInline)}</li>)}</Tag>;
    }
    // ...exhaustive; TS will error if you miss a case
  }
}
```

The exhaustiveness check is the payoff: add a new block type and the compiler tells you every renderer that needs updating.

# TypeScript Types for a Product Page

Here's a well-structured set of types. The key design decision is how to model the "long description with formatting" — I'll use a **block-based rich content model**, which is type-safe, renderer-agnostic, and easy to extend.

```ts
// ---------- Primitives ----------

/** ISO 4217 currency code, e.g. "USD", "EUR", "JPY". */
export type CurrencyCode = string;

/** Monetary amount. Store as integer minor units (cents) to avoid float errors. */
export interface Money {
  /** Amount in the smallest currency unit, e.g. 1999 = $19.99 */
  amount: number;
  currency: CurrencyCode;
}

// ---------- Images ----------

export interface ProductImage {
  id: string;
  url: string;
  /** Alt text for accessibility. */
  alt: string;
  /** Intrinsic dimensions — helps prevent layout shift. */
  width: number;
  height: number;
  /** Optional low-res placeholder (LQIP / blurhash data URI). */
  placeholder?: string;
}

// ---------- Rich description ----------

/** Inline text with optional styling. */
export interface TextSpan {
  type: "text";
  value: string;
  bold?: boolean;
  italic?: boolean;
  underline?: boolean;
  code?: boolean;
  /** Optional link target. */
  href?: string;
}

export type InlineNode = TextSpan | { type: "lineBreak" };

/** A paragraph of inline content. */
export interface ParagraphBlock {
  type: "paragraph";
  children: InlineNode[];
}

export interface HeadingBlock {
  type: "heading";
  /** 1–6, mirroring HTML heading levels. */
  level: 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6;
  children: InlineNode[];
}

export interface ListBlock {
  type: "list";
  ordered: boolean;
  items: InlineNode[][];
}

export interface QuoteBlock {
  type: "quote";
  children: InlineNode[];
  attribution?: string;
}

export interface ImageBlock {
  type: "image";
  image: ProductImage;
  caption?: string;
}

export interface CodeBlock {
  type: "code";
  language?: string;
  code: string;
}

export interface DividerBlock {
  type: "divider";
}

/** Discriminated union — exhaustive switches stay type-safe. */
export type DescriptionBlock =
  | ParagraphBlock
  | HeadingBlock
  | ListBlock
  | QuoteBlock
  | ImageBlock
  | CodeBlock
  | DividerBlock;

/** The formatted long description. */
export interface RichDescription {
  /** Plain-text fallback for SEO, search, and screen readers. */
  plainText: string;
  blocks: DescriptionBlock[];
}

// ---------- Product ----------

export interface Product {
  id: string;
  /** URL-safe identifier, e.g. "classic-leather-wallet". */
  slug: string;
  name: string;
  /** Optional subtitle / short tagline shown under the name. */
  tagline?: string;
  price: Money;
  /** Original price, if the product is on sale. */
  compareAtPrice?: Money;
  /** Primary image first; the rest form the gallery. */
  images: ProductImage[];
  description: RichDescription;
}
```

## Why these choices

**`Money` as an object, not a `number`.** A bare `number` for price forces you to guess the currency and invites floating-point bugs. Storing minor units (cents) as an integer is the standard approach used by Stripe, Shopify, etc.

**Discriminated union for blocks.** Each block variant carries exactly the fields it needs. When you write a renderer:

```ts
function renderBlock(block: DescriptionBlock) {
  switch (block.type) {
    case "paragraph":   return <p>{renderInline(block.children)}</p>;
    case "heading":     return <H level={block.level}>{renderInline(block.children)}</H>;
    case "list":        return renderList(block.ordered, block.items);
    case "quote":       return <blockquote>…</blockquote>;
    case "image":       return <img src={block.image.url} alt={block.image.alt} />;
    case "code":        return <pre><code>{block.code}</code></pre>;
    case "divider":     return <hr />;
    // TypeScript will error here if you forget a case
  }
}
```

**`plainText` fallback.** Rich content is bad for SEO and search. Keeping a plain-text version alongside the blocks lets you feed it to meta tags, search indexes, and screen readers without re-deriving it every render.

**`InlineNode` vs. `DescriptionBlock`.** Inline content (bold, links) lives inside blocks; blocks live inside the description. Keeping these separate prevents invalid nesting like a heading inside a link.

## Alternatives worth considering

- **If the description comes from a CMS** (Sanity, Contentful, Strapi), it often already ships as Portable Text or a similar JSON structure — mirror that shape instead of inventing a new one.
- **If you'd rather store HTML or Markdown**, replace `RichDescription` with `{ html: string }` or `{ markdown: string }` and sanitize/render at the edge. Simpler, but you lose type-level safety on the content shape.
- **If you need variants** (size, color), add a `variants: ProductVariant[]` field where each variant has its own `price`, `sku`, and `inStock`.

```ts
export interface ProductPage {
  name: string;
  price: Price;
  images: ProductImage[];
  longDescription: RichTextDocument;
}

export interface Price {
  /** Amount in minor units, e.g. 1999 = $19.99 */
  amount: number;
  /** ISO 4217 currency code, e.g. "USD", "EUR" */
  currency: string;
  /** Optional original price for showing discounts */
  compareAtAmount?: number;
}

export interface ProductImage {
  url: string;
  alt: string;
  width?: number;
  height?: number;
}

/* ----------------------------- Rich Text ----------------------------- */

export type RichTextDocument = RichTextBlock[];

export type RichTextBlock =
  | ParagraphBlock
  | HeadingBlock
  | ListBlock
  | QuoteBlock
  | CodeBlock
  | ImageBlock;

interface BlockBase {
  key: string;
}

export interface ParagraphBlock extends BlockBase {
  type: "paragraph";
  children: RichTextInline[];
}

export interface HeadingBlock extends BlockBase {
  type: "heading";
  level: 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6;
  children: RichTextInline[];
}

export interface ListBlock extends BlockBase {
  type: "list";
  style: "bullet" | "number";
  items: ListItem[];
}

export interface ListItem {
  key: string;
  children: RichTextInline[];
}

export interface QuoteBlock extends BlockBase {
  type: "quote";
  children: RichTextInline[];
  attribution?: string;
}

export interface CodeBlock extends BlockBase {
  type: "code";
  language?: string;
  code: string;
}

export interface ImageBlock extends BlockBase {
  type: "image";
  image: ProductImage;
  caption?: RichTextInline[];
}

export type RichTextInline =
  | TextInline
  | LinkInline
  | StrongInline
  | EmphasisInline
  | CodeInline
  | LineBreakInline;

interface InlineBase {
  key: string;
}

export interface TextInline extends InlineBase {
  type: "text";
  text: string;
}

export interface LinkInline extends InlineBase {
  type: "link";
  href: string;
  children: RichTextInline[];
}

export interface StrongInline extends InlineBase {
  type: "strong";
  children: RichTextInline[];
}

export interface EmphasisInline extends InlineBase {
  type: "emphasis";
  children: RichTextInline[];
}

export interface CodeInline extends InlineBase {
  type: "code";
  code: string;
}

export interface LineBreakInline extends InlineBase {
  type: "lineBreak";
}
```

This models the long description as structured rich text, so you can support paragraphs, headings, lists, quotes, code, images, links, bold, italics, and inline code.

## Core model

```ts
/* ---------- Branded IDs (prevents mixing up id types) ---------- */
type Brand<T, B extends string> = T & { readonly __brand: B };

export type PostId   = Brand<string, 'PostId'>;
export type AuthorId = Brand<string, 'AuthorId'>;
export type TagId    = Brand<string, 'TagId'>;

/* ---------- Author ---------- */
export interface Author {
  id: AuthorId;
  name: string;
  email?: string;
  bio?: string;
  avatarUrl?: string;
  website?: string;
}

/* ---------- Tag ---------- */
export interface Tag {
  id: TagId;
  slug: string;   // URL-safe, unique: "typescript"
  name: string;   // display label: "TypeScript"
}

/* ---------- Body (discriminated by format) ---------- */
export type PostBody =
  | { format: 'plain';    text: string }
  | { format: 'markdown'; markdown: string }
  | { format: 'html';     html: string };

/* ---------- Post ---------- */
export type PostStatus = 'draft' | 'published' | 'archived';

export interface BlogPost {
  id: PostId;
  title: string;
  slug: string;              // unique, URL-safe
  author: Author;            // embedded (see note below)
  tags: Tag[];               // embedded (see note below)
  body: PostBody;
  excerpt?: string;          // short summary for listings / SEO
  status: PostStatus;
  publishedAt?: string;      // ISO 8601; set when status === 'published'
  createdAt: string;         // ISO 8601
  updatedAt: string;         // ISO 8601
}
```

## Normalized variant (for storage / API persistence)

Keep relations as IDs in the database, and hydrate them for read APIs:

```ts
export interface BlogPostRecord {
  id: PostId;
  title: string;
  slug: string;
  authorId: AuthorId;
  tagIds: TagId[];
  body: PostBody;
  excerpt?: string;
  status: PostStatus;
  publishedAt?: string;
  createdAt: string;
  updatedAt: string;
}

/** Hydrated view returned to clients */
export type BlogPostView = BlogPost;
```

## Example

```ts
const post = {
  id: 'p_01' as PostId,
  title: 'Designing TypeScript Data Models',
  slug: 'designing-typescript-data-models',
  author: {
    id: 'a_01' as AuthorId,
    name: 'Ada Lovelace',
    email: 'ada@example.com',
  },
  tags: [
    { id: 't_01' as TagId, slug: 'typescript', name: 'TypeScript' },
    { id: 't_02' as TagId, slug: 'web-dev', name: 'Web Dev' },
  ],
  body: {
    format: 'markdown',
    markdown: '# Hello\n\nSome **content**.',
  },
  status: 'published',
  publishedAt: '2024-05-01T09:00:00.000Z',
  createdAt: '2024-04-28T12:00:00.000Z',
  updatedAt: '2024-05-01T09:00:00.000Z',
} satisfies BlogPost;
```

## Design notes

| Decision | Rationale |
|---|---|
| **Branded IDs** | `PostId` and `AuthorId` are both `string` at runtime, but the brand stops you from passing an author ID where a post ID is expected. |
| **Discriminated `PostBody`** | Narrowing on `body.format` gives you `body.markdown` only for markdown posts — no invalid field access. |
| **Embedded vs. referenced** | Embedding `author`/`tags` is convenient for read-heavy APIs (one fetch, no N+1). Use `BlogPostRecord` with `authorId`/`tagIds` for storage, then hydrate. |
| **ISO strings, not `Date`** | Keeps the model JSON-serializable for REST/GraphQL. Use `Date` if the model never crosses a network boundary. |
| **`slug` + `title`** | `title` is for humans, `slug` is for URLs; they diverge (punctuation, unicode, length limits). |
| **`status` + `publishedAt`** | `publishedAt` is optional because drafts have no publish date; enforce the invariant in your validation layer. |

## Runtime validation

TypeScript types vanish at runtime — validate untrusted input (API bodies, DB rows) with a schema library:

```ts
import { z } from 'zod';

const AuthorSchema = z.object({
  id: z.string(),
  name: z.string().min(1),
  email: z.string().email().optional(),
});

const TagSchema = z.object({
  id: z.string(),
  slug: z.string().regex(/^[a-z0-9-]+$/),
  name: z.string().min(1),
});

const PostBodySchema = z.discriminatedUnion('format', [
  z.object({ format: z.literal('plain'),    text: z.string() }),
  z.object({ format: z.literal('markdown'), markdown: z.string() }),
  z.object({ format: z.literal('html'),     html: z.string() }),
]);

export const BlogPostSchema = z.object({
  id: z.string(),
  title: z.string().min(1).max(200),
  slug: z.string().regex(/^[a-z0-9-]+$/),
  author: AuthorSchema,
  tags: z.array(TagSchema),
  body: PostBodySchema,
  excerpt: z.string().optional(),
  status: z.enum(['draft', 'published', 'archived']),
  publishedAt: z.string().datetime().optional(),
  createdAt: z.string().datetime(),
  updatedAt: z.string().datetime(),
});
```

**Simplified alternative** — if you don't need tags as first-class entities, `tags: string[]` and `author: string` (a name) are perfectly reasonable for a small app; the richer model above pays off once tags need slugs, descriptions, or their own pages.

# Blog Post Data Model (TypeScript)

## Option 1: Simple / Flat Model

Best for small apps, prototypes, or when the API returns denormalized data.

```ts
export interface BlogPost {
  id: string;
  title: string;
  author: string;
  tags: string[];
  body: string;
}
```

**Example:**

