# Blog Post Data Model (TypeScript)

## 1. Design decisions at a glance

| Concern | Decision | Why |
|---|---|---|
| IDs | Branded `string` types | Prevents passing an `AuthorId` where a `PostId` is expected |
| Author | Referenced by ID (normalized) | Avoids duplicating author data across every post |
| Tags | Referenced by ID, with a separate `Tag` registry | Same reason; also lets you rename a tag in one place |
| Body | Markdown string (simple) *or* block union (rich) | Pick one; don't mix |
| Dates | ISO 8601 `string` | JSON-safe, sortable, no `Date` serialization surprises |
| Status | Discriminated union | The compiler enforces "published ⇒ has `publishedAt`" |

---

## 2. Core types

```ts
// ---------- Branded IDs ----------
type Brand<T, B extends string> = T & { readonly __brand: B };

export type PostId  = Brand<string, 'PostId'>;
export type AuthorId = Brand<string, 'AuthorId'>;
export type TagId   = Brand<string, 'TagId'>;

/** ISO 8601 timestamp, e.g. "2024-03-11T09:30:00.000Z" */
export type IsoDateTime = Brand<string, 'IsoDateTime'>;

// ---------- Author ----------
export interface Author {
  id: AuthorId;
  /** URL-safe handle, e.g. "ada-lovelace" */
  slug: string;
  name: string;
  email?: string;
  avatarUrl?: string;
  bio?: string;
  links?: {
    website?: string;
    twitter?: string;
    github?: string;
  };
}

// ---------- Tag ----------
export interface Tag {
  id: TagId;
  /** Lowercase, hyphenated, unique — used in URLs */
  slug: string;
  /** Human-readable display name, e.g. "TypeScript" */
  label: string;
  description?: string;
}
```

---

## 3. Body: two viable representations

### Option A — Markdown string (most blogs)

```ts
export interface MarkdownBody {
  format: 'markdown';
  /** Raw markdown source */
  content: string;
}
```

### Option B — Structured blocks (rich content, WYSIWYG editors)

```ts
export type ContentBlock =
  | { type: 'paragraph'; text: string }
  | { type: 'heading'; level: 2 | 3 | 4; text: string }
  | { type: 'code'; language: string; code: string }
  | { type: 'quote'; text: string; attribution?: string }
  | { type: 'image'; src: string; alt: string; caption?: string }
  | { type: 'list'; ordered: boolean; items: string[] }
  | { type: 'embed'; provider: 'youtube' | 'twitter' | 'codepen'; url: string };

export interface BlockBody {
  format: 'blocks';
  blocks: ContentBlock[];
}

export type PostBody = MarkdownBody | BlockBody;
```

> **Tip:** make `format` a discriminant so consumers can `switch (body.format)` and get exhaustiveness checking.

---

## 4. The `Post` entity

```ts
interface PostBase {
  id: PostId;
  title: string;
  /** URL-safe, unique, e.g. "designing-a-data-model" */
  slug: string;
  /** FK → Author.id */
  authorId: AuthorId;
  /** FKs → Tag.id */
  tagIds: TagId[];
  body: PostBody;
  /** Short summary for listings / meta description */
  excerpt?: string;
  createdAt: IsoDateTime;
  updatedAt: IsoDateTime;
  seo?: {
    metaTitle?: string;
    metaDescription?: string;
    canonicalUrl?: string;
    ogImageUrl?: string;
  };
}

/** Status as a discriminated union so invalid states can't be constructed */
export type Post =
  | (PostBase & { status: 'draft' })
  | (PostBase & { status: 'published'; publishedAt: IsoDateTime })
  | (PostBase & { status: 'archived'; archivedAt: IsoDateTime });
```

### Convenience aliases

```ts
export type PublishedPost = Extract<Post, { status: 'published' }>;

/** Payload for creating a post */
export type PostCreate = Omit<PostBase, 'id' | 'createdAt' | 'updatedAt'> & {
  status?: 'draft';
};

/** Payload for updating a post */
export type PostUpdate = Partial<Omit<PostBase, 'id' | 'createdAt'>>;
```

---

## 5. Read model (hydrated for the UI)

Keep the write model normalized, but expose a denormalized view for rendering:

```ts
export interface PostView extends Omit<PostBase, 'authorId' | 'tagIds'> {
  author: Author;
  tags: Tag[];
  status: Post['status'];
  publishedAt?: IsoDateTime;
}
```

```ts
// Usage
const view: PostView = {
  ...post,
  author: await authors.find(post.authorId),
  tags: await tags.findMany(post.tagIds),
};
```

---

## 6. Runtime validation (types vanish at runtime)

```ts
import { z } from 'zod';

const isoDateTime = z.string().datetime({ offset: true });

export const PostSchema = z.object({
  id: z.string().min(1),
  title: z.string().min(1).max(120),
  slug: z.string().regex(/^[a-z0-9]+(?:-[a-z0-9]+)*$/, 'must be kebab-case'),
  authorId: z.string().min(1),
  tagIds: z.array(z.string()).max(10),
  body: z.discriminatedUnion('format', [
    z.object({ format: z.literal('markdown'), content: z.string().min(1) }),
    z.object({ format: z.literal('blocks'), blocks: z.array(z.unknown()).min(1) }),
  ]),
  excerpt: z.string().max(300).optional(),
  status: z.enum(['draft', 'published', 'archived']),
  publishedAt: isoDateTime.optional(),
  archivedAt: isoDateTime.optional(),
  createdAt: isoDateTime,
  updatedAt: isoDateTime,
}).superRefine((post, ctx) => {
  if (post.status === 'published' && !post.publishedAt) {
    ctx.addIssue({
      code: z.ZodIssueCode.custom,
      path: ['publishedAt'],
      message: 'publishedAt is required when status is "published"',
    });
  }
});

export type PostInput = z.infer<typeof PostSchema>;
```

---

## 7. Gotchas & notes

- **Branded types need casting at boundaries.** Parse untrusted input with Zod, then cast: `id: parsed.id as PostId`. A small helper keeps this tidy:
  ```ts
  export const asPostId = (v: string) => v as PostId;
  ```
- **Tag normalization.** Normalize on write, not read: lowercase, trim, dedupe, cap the count (e.g. 10). Store the canonical `Tag` rows in a registry so `tagIds` never dangle.
- **Slug uniqueness.** Enforce with a DB unique index; the type system can't help here.
- **If you use `Date` instead of ISO strings**, define a separate DTO type for the wire format — `Date` serializes to a string in JSON and silently breaks `instanceof` checks after a round-trip.
- **Prefer `readonly` on stored entities** to prevent accidental mutation of shared references:
  ```ts
  export interface Author { readonly id: AuthorId; /* ... */ }
  ```
- **Versioning.** Add `version: number` to `PostBase` for optimistic concurrency control if posts can be edited concurrently.

# Blog Post Data Model (TypeScript)

## 1. Core primitives

```ts
/** Branded types prevent accidentally passing a PostId where a TagId is expected. */
declare const brand: unique symbol;
type Brand<T, B extends string> = T & { readonly [brand]: B };

export type PostId = Brand<string, "PostId">;
export type AuthorId = Brand<string, "AuthorId">;
export type TagId = Brand<string, "TagId">;

/** ISO-8601 UTC timestamp, e.g. "2024-03-01T12:00:00.000Z" */
export type IsoDateString = Brand<string, "IsoDateString">;
```

## 2. Supporting entities

```ts
export interface Author {
  id: AuthorId;
  /** Display name shown in byline. */
  name: string;
  /** Optional handle/slug, e.g. "jane-doe". */
  slug?: string;
  email?: string;
  avatarUrl?: string;
  bio?: string;
  /** Links shown on the post page. */
  links?: {
    website?: string;
    twitter?: string;
    github?: string;
    mastodon?: string;
  };
}

/**
 * Tags are modeled as a separate collection (normalized) so they can be
 * renamed/merged without rewriting every post. See §5 for the denormalized
 * alternative.
 */
export interface Tag {
  id: TagId;
  /** URL-safe identifier, unique across the tag collection. */
  slug: string;
  /** Human-readable label, e.g. "TypeScript". */
  label: string;
  description?: string;
}
```

## 3. Body

```ts
export type PostBodyFormat = "markdown" | "html" | "portable-text";

/**
 * The body is stored as a raw string in its source format. Rendering is a
 * separate concern — see `renderBody` in §6.
 */
export interface PostBody {
  format: PostBodyFormat;
  /** Raw source text in the declared format. */
  content: string;
  /** Optional plain-text excerpt, precomputed for search & previews. */
  excerpt?: string;
  /** Optional word count, precomputed for reading-time estimates. */
  wordCount?: number;
}
```

## 4. The post

```ts
export type PostStatus = "draft" | "review" | "scheduled" | "published" | "archived";

export interface PostMeta {
  /** Short summary used for SEO meta description and social cards. */
  summary?: string;
  /** Canonical URL path, e.g. "/blog/typescript-data-models". */
  slug: string;
  /** Cover image for the social card / hero banner. */
  coverImageUrl?: string;
  /** Set when the post is a rewrite of another post. */
  canonicalUrl?: string;
  /** SEO overrides; falls back to derived values at render time. */
  seo?: {
    title?: string;
    description?: string;
    ogImageUrl?: string;
    noindex?: boolean;
  };
}

export interface Post {
  id: PostId;
  title: string;
  /** Denormalized for display convenience; `authorId` remains the source of truth. */
  author: Author;
  /** Normalized tag references. */
  tags: Tag[];
  body: PostBody;
  status: PostStatus;
  meta: PostMeta;
  createdAt: IsoDateString;
  updatedAt: IsoDateString;
  publishedAt?: IsoDateString;
  /** Date the post is scheduled to publish (only meaningful when status = "scheduled"). */
  scheduledAt?: IsoDateString;
}
```

### Status as a discriminated union

If you want the compiler to enforce that published posts have a `publishedAt` and scheduled posts have a `scheduledAt`, model status as a union of variants:

```ts
interface PostBase {
  id: PostId;
  title: string;
  author: Author;
  tags: Tag[];
  body: PostBody;
  meta: PostMeta;
  createdAt: IsoDateString;
  updatedAt: IsoDateString;
}

export type Post =
  | (PostBase & { status: "draft" | "review"; publishedAt?: never; scheduledAt?: never })
  | (PostBase & { status: "scheduled"; scheduledAt: IsoDateString; publishedAt?: never })
  | (PostBase & { status: "published"; publishedAt: IsoDateString; scheduledAt?: never })
  | (PostBase & { status: "archived"; publishedAt: IsoDateString; scheduledAt?: never });
```

## 5. Normalized vs. embedded tags

| Approach | Shape | Use when |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Normalized** (above) | `Post.tags: Tag[]`, `Tag` stored in its own collection | Tags have their own pages, descriptions, or counts |
| **Embedded** | `Post.tags: string[]` (slugs) | Simple blogs, no tag landing pages |
| **Hybrid** | `Post.tagIds: TagId[]` + a `tagsById` map in the store | Large datasets, avoids duplicating tag objects per post |

Hybrid example:

```ts
export interface PostRecord {
  id: PostId;
  title: string;
  authorId: AuthorId;
  tagIds: TagId[];
  body: PostBody;
  status: PostStatus;
  createdAt: IsoDateString;
  updatedAt: IsoDateString;
  publishedAt?: IsoDateString;
}

/** Assembles a full `Post` from a record + lookup tables. */
export function hydratePost(
  record: PostRecord,
  authorsById: Record<AuthorId, Author>,
  tagsById: Record<TagId, Tag>,
): Post {
  return {
    ...record,
    author: authorsById[record.authorId],
    tags: record.tagIds.map((id) => tagsById[id]),
  };
}
```

## 6. Derived helpers

```ts
export function readingTime(body: PostBody): number {
  const words = body.content.trim().split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean).length;
  return Math.max(1, Math.round(words / 220));
}

export function isVisible(post: Post, now: Date = new Date()): boolean {
  if (post.status === "published") return true;
  if (post.status === "scheduled" && post.scheduledAt) {
    return new Date(post.scheduledAt) <= now;
  }
  return false;
}
```

## 7. Runtime validation

TypeScript types vanish at runtime, so validate untrusted input (API payloads, CMS imports) with a schema library:

```ts
import { z } from "zod";

export const tagSchema = z.object({
  id: z.string().min(1),
  slug: z.string().regex(/^[a-z0-9]+(?:-[a-z0-9]+)*$/),
  label: z.string().min(1).max(50),
  description: z.string().max(280).optional(),
});

export const postSchema = z.object({
  id: z.string().min(1),
  title: z.string().min(1).max(200),
  author: z.object({
    id: z.string().min(1),
    name: z.string().min(1),
    slug: z.string().optional(),
  }),
  tags: z.array(tagSchema).max(10).default([]),
  body: z.object({
    format: z.enum(["markdown", "html", "portable-text"]),
    content: z.string().min(1),
  }),
  status: z.enum(["draft", "review", "scheduled", "published", "archived"]),
  createdAt: z.string().datetime(),
  updatedAt: z.string().datetime(),
  publishedAt: z.string().datetime().optional(),
  scheduledAt: z.string().datetime().optional(),
});

export type PostInput = z.infer<typeof postSchema>;
```

## Design notes

- **IDs are opaque strings**, not numbers — lets you migrate to UUIDs, ULIDs, or prefixed IDs (`post_01H...`) without a schema change.
- **Timestamps are UTC ISO strings** in storage; convert to `Date` only at the edges. This avoids timezone bugs across servers and keeps JSON-serializable records.
- **The body keeps its source format** so you can re-render (e.g. swap Markdown parsers) without data loss; store rendered HTML in a cache/CDN, not in the source of truth.
- **`status` is a state machine** — enforce legal transitions in your service layer (`draft → review → published → archived`), not just in the type.
- **Author is embedded** because a byline is read on nearly every post render; tags are normalized because they're shared, filtered, and renamed.

```typescript
/** A tag attached to a blog post. */
export interface Tag {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  slug: string;
}

/** The author of a blog post. */
export interface Author {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  email?: string;
  bio?: string;
  avatarUrl?: string;
}

/** A blog post. */
export interface BlogPost {
  id: string;
  title: string;
  author: Author;
  tags: Tag[];
  body: string;
  /** ISO 8601 date string, e.g. "2024-01-31T12:00:00Z". */
  publishedAt?: string;
  createdAt: string;
  updatedAt: string;
  excerpt?: string;
  draft?: boolean;
}
```

**Notes**

- `body` is a plain string (e.g., Markdown or HTML). For structured content, swap it for a richer type:
  ```typescript
  type BodyBlock =
    | { type: "paragraph"; text: string }
    | { type: "heading"; level: 1 | 2 | 3; text: string }
    | { type: "code"; language: string; code: string }
    | { type: "image"; url: string; alt: string };

  interface BlogPost {
    // ...
    body: BodyBlock[];
  }
  ```
- `author` is embedded as an object. If posts are stored in a relational database and authors live in their own table, use `authorId: string` instead and resolve the author at read time.
- `tags` is an array so posts can have zero or many tags. Use `readonly Tag[]` if you want to prevent mutation of the array reference.

## Recommended Model (normalized, reusable)

```ts
export interface Author {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  email?: string;
  bio?: string;
  avatarUrl?: string;
}

export interface Tag {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  slug: string;
}

export interface BlogPost {
  id: string;
  title: string;
  author: Author;
  tags: Tag[];
  body: string;
  slug?: string;
  publishedAt?: string; // ISO 8601 timestamp
  updatedAt?: string;   // ISO 8601 timestamp
}
```

**Example:**

```ts
const post: BlogPost = {
  id: "post_123",
  title: "Understanding TypeScript's Structural Typing",
  author: {
    id: "author_1",
    name: "Ada Lovelace",
    email: "ada@example.com",
  },
  tags: [
    { id: "tag_1", name: "TypeScript", slug: "typescript" },
    { id: "tag_2", name: "Types", slug: "types" },
  ],
  body: "Structural typing is one of TypeScript's most distinctive features...",
  slug: "understanding-structural-typing",
  publishedAt: "2024-03-15T09:30:00Z",
};
```

## Simplified Variant (embedded/denormalized)

For small apps or API payloads where the author and tags don't need independent lookup:

```ts
export interface BlogPost {
  id: string;
  title: string;
  author: string;   // display name or author ID
  tags: string[];   // tag names or slugs
  body: string;
  publishedAt?: string;
}
```

## Design Notes

- **Author as an object vs. string:** Use an object when authors appear across many posts (avoids repeating name/bio, allows profile links). Use a plain string when posts are standalone records.
- **Tags as objects vs. strings:** Object tags support display names that differ from slugs (`"TypeScript"` → `typescript`) and let you attach tag metadata. Plain strings are fine for simple filters.
- **Dates as `string`:** ISO 8601 strings serialize cleanly to JSON; use `Date` only if you control the boundary where data enters your app.
- **Immutability:** For read-heavy data (e.g., fetched into Redux/state), consider `readonly tags: readonly Tag[]` to prevent accidental mutation.
- **Body format:** `string` assumes plain text/Markdown. If you need rich content, swap it for a discriminated union:

```ts
export type PostBody =
  | { type: "markdown"; content: string }
  | { type: "html"; content: string }
  | { type: "blocks"; content: ContentBlock[] };
```

- **Validation:** These types are compile-time only — pair them with a runtime schema (Zod, Valibot) if data comes from an untrusted source (API, user input).

