## Design notes

- **Branded IDs** stop `author.id` being passed where `post.id` belongs — a cheap win with zero runtime cost.
- **`format` as a discriminant** lets you swap Markdown and block bodies without changing the rest of the model.
- **Denormalized `author`** avoids an N+1 on list views; keep `authorId` around if you also need writes.
- **Separate `createdAt` / `publishedAt`** — drafts have no publish date, and the `status` discriminant makes that explicit.
- **Runtime schema** is required if data crosses a network boundary; TypeScript types vanish at runtime.
- Consider adding `seo: { metaTitle?, metaDescription?, canonicalUrl? }` and `coverImage?: { src: URL; alt: string }` for production blogs.

## Core Model

```ts
// ---------- Branded IDs (prevent mixing up entity references) ----------
type Brand<T, B extends string> = T & { readonly __brand: B };

export type PostId = Brand<string, "PostId">;
export type AuthorId = Brand<string, "AuthorId">;
export type TagId = Brand<string, "TagId">;

// ---------- Author ----------
export interface Author {
  id: AuthorId;
  name: string;
  /** Canonical URL of the author's profile page. */
  url?: string;
  avatarUrl?: string;
  bio?: string;
}

// ---------- Tag ----------
export interface Tag {
  id: TagId;
  /** URL-safe slug, e.g. "typescript". Unique per blog. */
  slug: string;
  /** Human-readable label, e.g. "TypeScript". */
  label: string;
}

// ---------- Body ----------
/**
 * Body is stored as Markdown source. `contentHash` lets you detect
 * changes without re-parsing; `renderedHtml` is a cached artifact.
 */
export interface PostBody {
  /** Raw Markdown source — the single source of truth. */
  markdown: string;
  /** Optional pre-rendered HTML, regenerated when `markdown` changes. */
  renderedHtml?: string;
}

// ---------- Post ----------
export interface Post {
  id: PostId;
  title: string;
  /** Denormalized author snapshot for display; `authorId` is the FK. */
  author: Author;
  tags: Tag[];
  body: PostBody;

  /** Publication lifecycle. */
  status: "draft" | "published" | "archived";
  publishedAt?: string;   // ISO 8601
  updatedAt: string;
  /** Short summary used in listings and meta tags. */
  excerpt?: string;
  slug: string;
}
```

## Design Notes

**Why `author` is embedded rather than just `authorId`?** A blog post almost always needs the author's name next to it (byline, RSS feed, cards). Embedding a small snapshot avoids an N+1 lookup on every render. The tradeoff is staleness — if the author renames themselves, past posts keep the old name. If you'd rather have live data, drop the `author` field and use `authorId` + a join.

**Why are `tags` objects and not `string[]`?** A `string[]` is simpler but loses the display label (`"ts"` vs `"TypeScript"`), lets you create `"typescript"` and `"TypeScript"` as separate tags, and gives you nowhere to hang a tag description. If your tags are strictly free-form and never displayed separately, `tags: string[]` is fine — swap it in.

**Why is `body` an object?** A bare `body: string` is the simplest option and totally valid. Wrapping it lets you attach the rendered HTML and a content hash, which matters for caching and for change detection in an editor UI.

## Variants

**If you need rich, structured content** (embeds, code blocks with metadata, callouts):

```ts
type ContentBlock =
  | { type: "paragraph"; text: string }
  | { type: "heading"; level: 1 | 2 | 3 | 4; text: string }
  | { type: "code"; language: string; code: string }
  | { type: "image"; src: string; alt: string; caption?: string }
  | { type: "quote"; text: string; attribution?: string }
  | { type: "list"; ordered: boolean; items: string[] }
  | { type: "embed"; provider: "youtube" | "twitter" | "codepen"; url: string };

export interface Post {
  // ...rest of fields
  body: { blocks: ContentBlock[] };
}
```

**If you want runtime validation** (e.g. validating API input), pair the types with a schema:

```ts
import { z } from "zod";

export const PostSchema = z.object({
  id: z.string(),
  title: z.string().min(1).max(200),
  author: z.object({
    id: z.string(),
    name: z.string().min(1),
    url: z.string().url().optional(),
    avatarUrl: z.string().url().optional(),
    bio: z.string().optional(),
  }),
  tags: z.array(z.object({
    id: z.string(),
    slug: z.string().regex(/^[a-z0-9-]+$/),
    label: z.string().min(1),
  })).max(10),
  body: z.object({
    markdown: z.string(),
    renderedHtml: z.string().optional(),
  }),
  status: z.enum(["draft", "published", "archived"]),
  publishedAt: z.string().datetime().optional(),
  updatedAt: z.string().datetime(),
  excerpt: z.string().max(300).optional(),
  slug: z.string().regex(/^[a-z0-9-]+$/),
});

// Infer the TS type from the schema — one source of truth.
export type Post = z.infer<typeof PostSchema>;
```

