# Blog Post Data Model (TypeScript)

## 1. Design decisions up front

| Concern | Choice | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Identity | Branded string IDs | `string` everywhere makes it easy to pass a `TagId` where a `PostId` is expected |
| Dates | Branded ISO-8601 strings | JSON-serializable, sortable, no `Date` timezone ambiguity in payloads |
| Body | Structured block document | Enables rendering to HTML/Markdown/React, plus safe sanitization and excerpt extraction |
| Tags | Hydrated objects (not ID arrays) | A read model should be self-contained; a separate stored form keeps the DB normalized |
| Status | Discriminated union | Makes `publishedAt` required only when `status === "published"` — enforced by the compiler, not by convention |

---

## 2. Primitive types

```ts
// Branded types: zero runtime cost, full compile-time safety.
declare const brand: unique symbol;
export type Brand<T, B extends string> = T & { readonly [brand]: B };

export type PostId = Brand<string, "PostId">;
export type AuthorId = Brand<string, "AuthorId">;
export type TagId = Brand<string, "TagId">;

/** ISO-8601 / RFC-3339 timestamp, e.g. "2024-03-08T09:30:00.000Z" */
export type ISODateString = Brand<string, "ISODateString">;

/** URL-safe slug: lowercase, hyphen-separated */
export type Slug = Brand<string, "Slug">;

export const asPostId = (v: string) => v as PostId;
export const asAuthorId = (v: string) => v as AuthorId;
export const asTagId = (v: string) => v as TagId;
export const asISODate = (v: string | Date) =>
  (typeof v === "string" ? v : v.toISOString()) as ISODateString;
export const asSlug = (v: string) => v as Slug;
```

